\par\par \b \uc2 ING\uc2 's Brzeski Ponders the Great German Mystery -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,643 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 November 2014\par 08:56\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 0756 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ]--It's refreshing when an economist admits he (or she) isn't totally sure of what's happening. \uc2 ING\uc2 's Carsten Brzeski said Friday that recently, "the German economy has been a mystery." On the one hand, there's "partly terrifying data", especially from industry, yet there's also "anecdotal evidence and undebatable solid economic fundamentals," that seem to tell another story. Still, "it would be naive to simply deny clearly disappointing data." But he said it's hard to know what's really going on in Europe's largest economy. The question is, is Germany's slowdown a result of volatility from one-offs like weather, vacations and school holidays or is it the result of weaknesses in trading partners and the end of a growth cycle? "In our view, it is a combination of the two," he said. Perhaps fittingly, he says calling 3Q GDP "is not an easy bet." todd.buell@wsj.com \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 0751 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ]--ECB President \uc2 Mario Draghi\uc2 's comments Thursday of returning the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's balance sheet to the near EUR3 trillion level seen in March 2012 put large scale buying of European Government bonds back on the cards for early 2015 says \uc2 Barclays\uc2 . The bank says that the current purchase programs won't be enough to reach the stated goal while the other contingency for expanding QE - a further worsening of the inflation outlook - will also likely be triggered. (nick.cawley@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0732 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ]---The ruble's rapid drop to fresh record lows, a process repeated almost daily for more than a month, brought panic onto the market, says VTB 24, a retail arm of Russia's No.2 lender VTB. "A further ruble weakening starts to threaten stability of the financial system," VTB 24 says. It expects that the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Russia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will have to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  the market soon to stabilize the ruble. Until then VTB 24 recommends scaling down exposure to shares in Russian non-export companies with big loans in foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . (andrey.ostroukh@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0724 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ]--German data nerds woke up this morning to a bit of a mixed bag. Industrial production on the whole was weaker than expected in September, printing a +1.4% on the month, disappointing the Wall Street Journal economist forecast of a 2.0%. However, after Thursday's small gain in industrial orders, a miss in production wasn't all that surprising. Plus, the key capital goods category rose by 4.5% in September, though that followed a decline of 7.9%, potentially signaling strength in the important heavy machinery sector. And, perhaps to be expected, Germany's mighty export sector may be back on track. It grew by 5.5% on the month in September, almost making up for the 5.8% drop in August that, along with other bad data for that month, scared investors. (todd.buell@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0713 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] India's economic growth likely slowed to 5.2% in the second quarter ending September 30 as below-normal rains hurt farm output and industrial activity lost momentum, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 DBS Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2 . The Indian economy grew by 5.7% in the first quarter, the quickest pace in more than two years. Moderation in government spending to meet fiscal consolidation efforts and a wider trade deficit, will also weigh on growth. "This poses modest downside risks to our FY GDP projection at 6.0%, which we will review after 2Q GDP release later this month," it adds.(Prasanta.sahu@wsj.com, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Twitter\f28 \uc2 \f2 : @pksahu9) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0709 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Indonesian companies' production costs will not increase if the government cuts fuel subsidies as companies don't use such fuel, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Franciscus Welirang\f28 \uc2 \f2 , a director with one of Indonesia's largest food producers, Indofood Sukses Makmur. "If companies say their costs will increase, they lie," Welirang adds, saying the sooner the government hikes fuel prices the better. His comments reflect rising support for the subsidy cut, which would free up tens of billions of dollars for the government each year. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank Indonesia\f28 \uc2 \f2  has estimated that a 3,000 rupiah/liter increase in subsidized gasoline and diesel fuel prices later this year will raise inflation close to 10% at year end, from 4.83% currently, but inflation is expected to head lower quickly. Still, a fuel price increase will likely meet strong opposition in parliament and may also spark street protests. Hundreds of students clashed with police earlier this week in central Indonesia's city of Makassar in a protest against the planned policy. President Widodo is expected to raise fuel prices within the next two weeks. (i-made.sentana@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0652 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] China shares reverse earlier gains in the afternoon session as news about a setback for Chinese companies' overseas expansion hit construction, steel and machinery sectors broadly, outweighing strength in financial stocks. AFP reports that Mexico will restart bidding on a multi-billion-dollar tender previously awarded to a Chinese-led consortium to build the country's first bullet train, casting a shadow on China's infrastructure construction plans overseas. The Shanghai Composite Index is last down 0.2% at 2419.99, after touching a high of 2454.42, the highest level since March 2012. \f28 \uc2 \f2 China Railway Construction\f28 \uc2 \f2  (601186.SH), a leading member of the consortium, tumbles 6.9% to 6.60 yuan. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Citic Securities\f28 \uc2 \f2  (600030.SH) gains 2.8% to 13.55 yuan. (amy.li@dowjones.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0634 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] \f28 \uc2 \f2 India's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may turn more dovish in the December 2 policy review but the governor may wait for more clarity on inflation before he cuts interest rates, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of America Merrill Lynch\f28 \uc2 \f2  in a note. "We are increasingly confident about our call for a \f28 \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of India\f28 \uc2 \f2  repo rate cut in February," it says. The house expects the RBI to achieve its inflation targets of 8% for January 2015 and 6% for January 2016. "The case for lower rates looks increasingly compelling," it says. It expects growth in September to fall to about 5% from 5.7% and imported inflation is dropping with oil prices stabilizing. (nupur.acharya@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0609 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Australian stocks climb 0.8% Friday after an assurance by the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  it's committed to stimulus measures offset a sobering read on the Australian economy by local policymakers. The benchmark S&P/ASX 200 index adds 43 points to 5549.1, ending a gentle week with little volatility. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Australia's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  forecast below-average growth in the resource-rich country, signalling interest rates would remain at record lows for some time yet. \f28 \uc2 \f2 BHP Billiton\f28 \uc2 \f2  (BHP.AU) adds 2.1% and Commonwealth Bank (CBA.AU) is up 1.9%. (djnews.sydney@dowjones.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0605 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barclays\f28 \uc2 \f2  says the strength in Malaysia's intermediate imports continues to point to robust exports for the fourth quarter, particularly non-commodity products. Intermediate imports--unfinished parts such as circuit boards and automotive components used in the final assembly of computers and cars--grew 11.2% on-year in September. This, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barclays\f28 \uc2 \f2  says, bodes well for exports growth. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barclays\f28 \uc2 \f2  forecasts Malaysia's gross domestic product to expand 5.9% in 2014, but adds that there are "modest downside risks" to its 2015 projection of 5.5% growth. Malaysia's September exports grew 2.0% from a year earlier, below market expectations, while imports expanded 1.1% on-year. A Wall Street Journal poll had predicted a median rise of 2.7% for exports and 8.9% for imports. (jason.ng@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0603 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The JPY may continue to show weakness up until the middle of 2015, even after a short-term adjustment, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Daiwa Securities\f28 \uc2 \f2  chief \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  analyst Yuji Kameoka in a report. The USD/JPY's move can be explained largely using a combination of risk tolerance (U.S. stocks P/E) and the spread of yields on U.S. and Japanese bonds. But the rapid USD/JPY gains since last week far exceeds this gauge because the BOJ's surprise move along with the GPIF's portfolio reshuffle. But the gap between the USD/JPY and its theoretically-reasonable value will likely narrow, making the pair to stop rising further from around 115. Even with a subsequent adjustment, however, "it is highly possible that the JPY's weakness will persist" as the global economic recovery creates a risk-on mood. The USD/JPY is now at 115.31. (hiroyuki.kachi@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor SXS \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0550 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Singapore's workers are more disengaged in the workplace and more likely to leave their jobs in the short term than the global average, according to a survey by \f28 \uc2 \f2 Towers Watson\f28 \uc2 \f2 . The professional services consultant finds that a third of Singapore workers are "disengaged" at work compared with a quarter globally, while 34% said they are likely to leave their employer in the next two years, compared with 26% globally. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Towers Watson\f28 \uc2 \f2  said the reasons are due to a disconnect between employers' and employees' measures of workplace attraction, with employers more focused on career advancement than other factors like pay and job security, which employees said they preferred. (jake.watts@wsj.com; @jmwatts_) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 November 07, 2014 02:56 ET (07:56 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020141107eab70019l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
