\par\par \b \uc2 Ruble Leaps As Investors Await Rates Move --  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Trading\b0\par\par\uc2 736 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Oktober 2014\par 23:49\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par C4\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    (FROM THE WALL STREET JOURNAL 10/31/14) \par    By Andrey Ostroukh and Ira Iosebashvili \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 MOSCOW -- Russia's battered ruble rallied to its biggest one-day gain against the U.S. dollar in nearly six years, on expectations the country's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is likely to take strong measures to shore up the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 Driving the rally was the belief that the Bank of Russia will announce the steps at a policy meeting Friday. Some analysts also believe the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  staged a huge \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday in a bid to protect the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has fallen 25% against the U.S. dollar so far this year, from further weakness.\par \par \uc2 The combination of falling oil prices and the impact of Western sanctions has contributed to an economic slowdown in Russia, prompting investors to sell ruble-denominated assets such as stocks and bonds. Russian companies' dollar purchases ahead of end-of-year debt repayments also have weighed on the ruble.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  almost daily this month to soften the decline, selling more than $28 billion since early October. But with the price of oil, Russia's chief export, down more than 20% since the start of 2014, the bank so far has been unable to stop the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's slide. The ruble has lost 5% against the dollar in October.\par \par \uc2 That is why some investors believe Bank of Russia likely is preparing more drastic measures.\par \par \uc2 "There are growing expectations that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will take some major action," said Jorge Mariscal, emerging-markets chief investment officer at \uc2 UBS\uc2  Wealth Management, which oversees about $1 trillion. Mr. Mariscal said he anticipates an interest-rate increase on Friday -- by as much as 1.00 percentage point from the current 8%.\par \par \uc2 In late New York trading Thursday, the ruble rose 3.9% to 41.57 rubles per dollar, according to ICAP, the steepest daily gain since January 2009. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit fresh record lows in the first hours of the Moscow session, pressured by persistent concerns about Russia's economy.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 World Bank\uc2  last month said Russia was "on the edge of recession" and cut its forecast for its economy over the next two years, saying growth will stagnate amid a lack of structural changes and Western sanctions over Russia's role in the Ukraine conflict. Increasing tensions with the West could push Russia's economy into a long downturn, the \uc2 World Bank\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 Thursday's rally may have been sparked by a large wave of ruble buying by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which may have decided on a more aggressive strategy for defending its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , analysts at Capital Economics wrote in a research note. "It's not quite clear what lies behind this movement, but the most obvious explanation is that policy makers have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market," it said.\par \par \uc2 Investors who had bet against the ruble were likely forced to reverse their trades and buy back the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , driving prices up further, said Kirill Grishanov, head of financial markets at Russian bank \uc2 Promsvyazbank\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "The incredible volatility . . . is a consequence of the fact that the market was too involved in betting on the ruble's weakness," Mr. Grishanov said.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is committed to allowing the ruble to trade freely against major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  next year, although some speculate that the move could come even earlier. It will retain the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if it sees risks to financial stability.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of Russia declined to comment, citing a policy of remaining quiet for a week ahead of key meetings.\par \par \uc2 The gloomy economic climate and falling ruble have driven capital out of the country, with Russia's economic ministry projecting as much as $120 billion in outflows this year.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Marsical of \uc2 UBS\uc2  said he is advising clients to avoid the ruble, believing it will fall further.\par \par \uc2 "The ruble is poised to stay volatile," said \uc2 Eric Fine\uc2 , a portfolio manager at \uc2 Van Eck Global\uc2 , which manages $32.3 billion. "Whether it gets stronger or weaker, I wouldn't own any Russian assets. not because they're not valuable . . . but the sanctions make it way too risky. The upside is not worth that downside." He says he exited from his position in Russian assets last December.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Nicole Hong contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 October 30, 2014 18:49 ET (22:49 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020141030eaau0054d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
