\par\par \b \uc2 Time For Bank of  \highlight22\uc2 Russia \highlight\uc2  To Change  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Rule -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,329 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Oktober 2014\par 15:44\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 1444 GMT Ruble has shed 6.6% versus the dollar so far this month despite the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  of as much as $23.1B. The bank's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  mechanism was initially designed to ease downside pressure on the ruble but recently has transformed into a tool that can be used if one bets on the ruble weakness, market players say. \uc2 Sberbank CIB\uc2  says it is time to change the rules of these \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "The final aim of such changes should be a transition to a free float regime." The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  plans to let the ruble float freely in early 2015 but currently sells $350M at the weak boundary of the ruble band versus the euro-dollar basket before shifting the boundary 5 kopecks higher. (andrey.ostroukh@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 10:43 EDT - Downside short-term risks for the manufacturing sector are growing considerably as new orders fall in September. "The weak September report on the demand for long-lasting (durable) goods suggests that the darkening world economic and geopolitical picture is having an impact on already fragile business and consumer confidence," says Cliff Waldman, director of economic studies at the MAPI Foundation, the research affiliate of the Manufacturers Alliance for Productivity and Innovation. Machinery orders contracted a hefty 2.8% in September, while fabricated metal and primary metals registered positive demand. (clare.ansberry@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 10:39 EDT - Today brings two contrasts in economic expectations. Consumer confidence jumps to its highest level since before the recession, boosted by a large gain in expectations. But earlier the Commerce Department reports new orders for core capital goods fell in September. Since businesses typically pull back on capex orders when they are uncertain about economic conditions, the drop in capital demand suggests that companies--unlike consumers--are concerned about the future. (kathleen.madigan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 10:25 EDT - Chile's industrial production in September posted its strongest growth rate this year, but the private-sector manufacturing society Sofofa says that shouldn't be taken as a sign the sector is recovering. It contends the 3.9% increase was due in part to seasonal factors and one-off calendar events while "investments continue showing important declines" and that "sales in the domestic market don't show growth" versus a year ago. Chile's economy has been hurt by lower metal prices and weaker domestic demand. (ryan.dube@wsj.com; @duberyan)\par \par \uc2 10:22 EDT - The \uc2 Richmond Fed\uc2 's top-line manufacturing index jumps to 20 in October from 14 in September as production, new orders and backlog post healthy gains this month. Employment slows a bit, although Richmond-area manufacturers expect more hiring in the future. (kathleen.madigan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1422 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ]--They say that even the longest journey begins with a single step and the same is true with the ECB's effort to up its balance sheet by between EUR700 billion and EUR1 trillion. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reported Tuesday that its consolidated balance sheet rose by EUR3 billion last week to EUR2.03 trillion. There's still some work to do, but eventually the increases will be more substantial. The balance sheet rises when banks borrow money from the ECB or when the ECB buys assets, two things the ECB's going to be doing more of soon in an effort to drive up inflation and growth in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bloc. The ECB has already started to lend funds to banks at four-year maturity on the condition that they pass them on to the real economy and it has also started buying some private sector assets. (todd.buell@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1410 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ]--Bunds and gilts are both lower on the day, dragged down by a combination of a weak \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Treasury\f28 \uc2 \f2  complex and positive stock markets. U.S. Treasurys are being marked down ahead of short- and medium-dated supply, commencing with $29 billion two year paper later in the session. In addition, European stock markets continue to shrug off their recent weakness. The two-day FOMC also starts later Tuesday, with analysts expecting QE to end this month. December Bunds are 0.24 lower at 150.37 and December gilts are 0.32 lower at 115.19. In the cash market, 10-year Bunds yield 0.885% and gilts 2.235%, both up a couple of bps on the day. (nick.cawley@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 10:08 EDT - Bond prices hit fresh session lows as the latest gauge of consumer confidence rises. The US Conference Board's consumer confidence this month increases to 94.5 from 89 in September, beating expectations. The data suggest sentiment toward US growth remains upbeat despite the recent market turmoil and struggling growth in the eurozone--another case for the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fed\f28 \uc2 \f2  to end QE. Ten-year notes now 10/32 lower, yielding 2.292%. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 10:04 EDT - Scotiabank Peru is forecasting that Peru's annual inflation rate will remain within the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's target range for a third-consecutive month in October, as the consumer price index is forecast to rise 0.2% in the month. The firm says this will increase the 12-month inflation rate to 2.9% from 2.7%, which is still within the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's target range of 1%-3%. The increase in October is expected due to higher food and fuel costs, firm says. It expects the annual inflation rate to remain within the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's limit in the coming months and finish 2014 at about 3%. (ryan.dube@wsj.com; @duberyan)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 09:53 EDT - \f28 \uc2 \f2 The Conference Board of Canada\f28 \uc2 \f2  says Canadian consumers appear to have taken notice of the stock market's travails in recent week, with its consumer confidence falling by 3.9 points to 84, the second consecutive fall and the fifth decline in the last six months. It says the balance of opinion on job creation was negative, meaning more surveyed expect job losses rather than gains, for the 21st consecutive month. Regional confidence levels also remain divergent, as index values in Western Canada continue to outpace those in the rest of the country, the board says. It will be interesting to see if that remains the case if lower oil prices persist. (don.curren@wsj.com; @dbcurren)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1353 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ]--\f28 \uc2 \f2 Hungary's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  left key interest rates unchanged Tuesday but Capital Economics reckons there's some chance it will hint at monetary easing ahead in its 1400 GMT statement, given inflation's current weakness (it was down 0.5% in September) and the prospect of slower growth. Capital economist \f28 \uc2 \f2 William Jackson\f28 \uc2 \f2  expects \f28 \uc2 \f2 CPI\f28 \uc2 \f2  to pick up as the impact of previous cuts in household utility prices wears off. (veronika.gulyas@wsj.com; @VeronikaGulyas1) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1330 GMT \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fitch Ratings\f28 \uc2 \f2  says prospects for medium-sized Italian banks remain negative, after the European regulators' health check. Adds that recently strengthened capital--these banks collectively sold around EUR11 billion in fresh shares this year--was not enough to compensate for further deterioration in asset quality and structurally weak profitability in their businesses, which are essentially commercial. However, the ratings firm says it won't immediately take any rating action on Italian banks, but that the assessment provided a substantial amount of new data which will feed into analysis of the banks it rates. (giovanni.legorano@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 October 28, 2014 10:44 ET (14:44 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020141028eaas002f9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
