\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Russia \highlight\uc2  CB Resumes  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2 - \uc2 Rabobank\uc2  - -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,604 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Oktober 2014\par 11:56\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 1056 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ]--The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  has resumed regular \b \highlight22\uc2 FX interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the persistent selling pressure on the ruble caused by a mix of soft oil prices and western sanctions over the crisis in Ukraine. Since the end of September/beginning of October, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  shifted the RUB trading corridor by RUB 2.65 to 38.05-47.05 and sold more than USD 18bn, says EM strategist Piotr Matys. Russian officials will have now to make crucial decision on their support for the ruble as officials said the rate corridor will be scrapped by the end of the year. "This would be consistent with the official long-term strategy to implement an inflation targeting regime with fully flexible exchange rate and more importantly the CBR may still conduct \b \highlight22\uc2 FX interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  if proved necessary," he adds. (chiara.albanese@wsj.com @chiaraalbanese) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 1052 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ]--Germany has had one quarter of growth contraction in 2Q, while the October Ifo confidence index (gloomy again) is a fresh concern for 4Q. Nordea's chief European analyst Holger Sandte, however, says recession is a risk, but, while debating its chances is nice for the media and the markets, it's slightly beside the point economically. That point, according to Mr. Sandte, is not whether the GDP is growing or falling a bit, but whether it is growing enough to create more jobs. (emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 6:36 EDT - After the biggest week in some time for US stocks to further erase recent weakness, index futures are modestly lower to start this 5-day period--which will be highlighted by the FOMC meeting. There's been at least a little question--some fueled by policy makers themselves--as to whether the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  should end QE3 this week, as has long been thought of as the timetable. Concerns about a lack of inflation remain with some, and of course any sign the punch bowl will stick around a while longer is welcome news for stock bulls as it adds to the cash which can be plowed into equities amid the persistent hunt for yield. Dow-industrial futures are down 15 points and the S&P 500 is off 3. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com; @kevinkingsbury)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1027 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ]--October's fall in the Ifo index to 103.2, from 104.7, was a blow to hopes that the German recovery would resume toward the end of the year, and will add to pressure on the government to consider a fiscal boost, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Jennifer McKeown\f28 \uc2 \f2 , senior European economist at Capital Economics. The index has now fallen for six months in a row, but October's decline was particularly disappointing after the rise in Germany's composite PMI in the same month, and recent falls in energy prices and the euro, which should have boosted sentiment, Ms. McKeown says. Capital Economics, for now, continues to expect the German GDP to rise by 1.3% in 2014 and 1.5% in 2015, but this won't be enough to ensure a strong recovery or eradicate the threat of deflation in the eurozone as a whole, she says, adding that the risks are to the downside. (emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1022 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ]--Brazil's service sector confidence increases, but at a low pace. Its confidence index was at 101.9 in October from 100.7 in September, says the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Getulio Vargas Foundation\f28 \uc2 \f2 , or FGV. "Despite the expansion, the general picture still shows a unfavorable scenario," said Silvio Sales, a consultant there. After expanding 2.5% in 2013, Brazil's economy is expected to growth less than 0.5% this year, hurting confidence among both business and consumers. (rogerio.jelmayer@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1010 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Rating agency Fitch raises its outlook on Thailand telco \f28 \uc2 \f2 Total Access Communications\f28 \uc2 \f2  (DTAC.TH) to positive from stable citing strong earnings growth as an offset for heavy investment in 3G network expansion. "The positive outlook indicates that an upgrade of DTAC's ratings could occur over the next 24 months if DTAC's earnings and operating cash flow continue to improve strongly as expected" and leverage remains low, Fitch says. DTAC stock trades 0.5% lower late Monday at 100.50 baht and has fallen 3.8% in the month-to-date. Despite those declines, the stock is 4.1% higher in the year-to-date. (jake.watts@wsj.com; \f28 \uc2 \f2 Twitter\f28 \uc2 \f2 : @jmwatts_) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1002 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ]--With Sweden dipping deeper into deflation (\f28 \uc2 \f2 CPI\f28 \uc2 \f2  last -0.4% yr-yr) \f28 \uc2 \f2 Morgan Stanley\f28 \uc2 \f2  expects the Riksbank to remain dovish Tuesday. As the markets have already priced in a rate cut which means they are zero-bound, and QE is not seen as an option given the small size of Sweden's bond market, a weak \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is the likely answer to try and increase inflation. The bank prefers the risk/reward of long USD/SEK rather than EUR/SEK, given the ECB is also expected to remain accommodative. USD/SEK now at 7.26, the bank has a 7.50 target with a stop-loss down at 7.15. Riksbank rate announcement 0830 GMT Tuesday, the DowJones survey forecast is for the main refi rate to be cut from 0.25% to 0.05%. gary.stride@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1001 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ]--After President \f28 \uc2 \f2 Dilma Rousseff\f28 \uc2 \f2  won a second term on Sunday, defeating the conservative A\u233\'00\'E9cio Neves, investors now focus on the economic policies of her the new mandate, mainly those on the fiscal side, aspoor performance in this area promoted a downgrade of country's sovereign rating earlier this year. It's now just one notch above junk bond status at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Standard & Poor's\f28 \uc2 \f2 . "Going into 2015, investors should not rule out a credit downgrade to sub-investment grade as a result of a deterioration in debt dynamics," says Daniel Tenengauzer head of emerging markets and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategy at \f28 \uc2 \f2 RBC Capital Markets\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (rogerio.jelmayer@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1001 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] For all of its technocratic and professional expertise, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Goldman Sachs\f28 \uc2 \f2  says Indonesia's new cabinet faces a bigger challenge: domestic politics. "An important area of uncertainty is how some of these new and inexperienced ministers will handle the pressure from the parliament and various interest groups," head Asia-Pacific economist \f28 \uc2 \f2 Andrew Tilton\f28 \uc2 \f2  writes in a research note. He points to ministers in trade, transportation, fisheries, communications, tourism, energy and SOEs with unknown political skills. Tilton reminds that appointments by President Joko Widodo aren't finished either: the attorney general, investment chief and possibly a chief of staff and new national police head are still on the way. The police chief and the attorney general, "in particular, are key posts in the effort to push for clean government," he writes. (ben.otto@wsj.com; benottoWSJ) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0957 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ]--It was ridiculous for the market to have fretted about the ECB (stress test) results, as the ECB is actively supporting the markets in the midst of a worrying eurozone economic slowdown, says Jean Maigrot, European equity investment leader at NewSmith, who has been waiting for this morning's relief rally. "And I have acted." Mr. Maigrot sold out long positions on \f28 \uc2 \f2 Commerzbank\f28 \uc2 \f2 , \f28 \uc2 \f2 Unicredit\f28 \uc2 \f2  and \f28 \uc2 \f2 ING\f28 \uc2 \f2  and is keeping shorts on \f28 \uc2 \f2 BBVA\f28 \uc2 \f2  and Santander. "Today's results are by no means the end of the story," he says. (chiara.albanese@wsj.com @chiaraalbanese) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0956 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The Indian government has begun the process for auctioning frequencies to cellphone operators. The Department of Telecommunications had invited bids for auction firms to help the federal government sell frequencies, the first step towards actual auctions. The upcoming sale is crucial for cellphone companies as well as the government. India's government aims to raise 454.71 billion rupees through the sale, to narrow its fiscal deficit. (krishna.jai@wsj.com; \f28 \uc2 \f2 Twitter\f28 \uc2 \f2 : @jaikrishna) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0955 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ]--The reelection of Brazilian President \f28 \uc2 \f2 Dilma Rousseff\f28 \uc2 \f2  for a second four-year term may prompt a drop in Brazilian asset prices, as she is not likely to promote a major shift in country's economic policy in order to revive tepid growth, says Neil Shearing, an economist at Capital Economics. "\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dilma Rousseff\f28 \uc2 \f2 's narrow victory in yesterday's presidential run-off in Brazil has dented hopes that the elections might deliver much-need economic policy reforms," said Mr. Shearing. "Growth in Ms. Rousseff's first term in office was already the weakest under any president since the early 1990s and, in the absence of a major shift in policy in her second term, we expect more of the same over the next four years. Stocks, bonds and the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  are likely to fall when financial markets open later today," he added. (rogerio.jelmayer@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 October 27, 2014 06:56 ET (10:56 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020141027eaar00160\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
