\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-Putin says "foolish" sanctions will not hold back Russia\b0\par\par\uc2 805 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Oktober 2014\par 15:05\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2014 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 * Putin shrugs off sanctions over Ukraine\par \par \uc2 * Says privatisation to continue, no \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions\par \par \uc2 * Economists fears stagnation, faster capital flight (Adds further comments, reaction)\par \par \uc2 By Darya Korsunskaya\par \par \uc2 MOSCOW, Oct 2 (Reuters) - President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  dismissed Western sanctions as "utter foolishness" on Thursday and said they would not stop Russia developing into a stronger economic power.\par \par \uc2 Attempting to take the higher ground in a speech to foreign and Russian investors, Putin said he was relaxed about the measures imposed on his country over Ukraine even though they had broken the fundamental principles of the global economy.\par \par \uc2 Addressing financiers worried about the weakening economy, capital flight and a possible increase in state \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , he said \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  was well placed to weather the storm.\par \par \uc2 "We truly want a country that is strong, flourishing, free and open to the world," he told the VTB Russia Calling investment conference, ruling out restrictions on capital and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements out of Russia.\par \par \uc2 Making clear his aim was to soothe worried investors, he later began a question-and-answer session by saying with a wry smile: "All I have to do is smile to show the devil is not as frightening as he seems."\par \par \uc2 Contradicting some businessmen who fear sanctions could drive Russia into a new period of isolation, Putin underlined that he saw the country as part of the world economy, and, compared with others, a member playing by the rules.\par \par \uc2 He said sanctions violated the principles of the \uc2 World Trade Organization\uc2 , undermined the credibility of international financial institutions and reserve \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and would cause long-term damage to the entire global economy.\par \par \uc2 "It is utter foolishness from those governments, who are limiting their business, preventing it working, reducing its competitiveness, freeing up niches for competitors on as promising a market as Russia," he said.\par \par \uc2 "I would like to hope that we can get over this period of misunderstanding," he said to applause.\par \par \uc2 CALM AND RELAXED\par \par \uc2 It was a calm performance by the 61-year-old president, who won popularity in his first spell as president from 2000 until 2008 by overseeing nearly a decade of economic growth.\par \par \uc2 Now some economists, including the \uc2 World Bank\uc2 , see Russia entering a period of near stagnation, with growth of the $2 trillion economy barely climbing above zero.\par \par \uc2 Inflation is running at an annual rate of almost 8 percent, the rouble has fallen around 17 percent against the U.S. dollar this year and the price of oil - vital to Russia's economy - has dropped below the point at which it balances its budget. Capital flight was $75 billion in the first half of the year.\par \par \uc2 Plenty of experts see more problems than Putin appears ready to acknowledge, including the absence of a clear strategy to pull the world's eighth largest economy around.\par \par \uc2 "Right now it is clear that in this crisis situation for business there is no clear 'Plan B' from the state," said Diana Kaplinskaya, an economic analyst.\par \par \uc2 Acknowledging the "difficult times", Putin said he was sure the sanctions would spur domestic growth and reduce Russia's dependence on imports, enabling the economy to develop.\par \par \uc2 He said he was confident that a recent rise in inflation, linked to Russia's restrictions on Western food imports as a response to sanctions, would be temporary given the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's policy - which is "balanced and flexible".\par \par \uc2 Putin reiterated the importance of developing ties with Russia's partners to the east, congratulating companies which had already done deals with Chinese companies and said Russia would aim to shift to national \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in trade.\par \par \uc2 In a message to owners of firms privatised in the 1990s, when the Soviet Union's collapse gave way to chaotic capitalism, he said there would not be a wide-scale revision of such sales.\par \par \uc2 A Moscow court's decision last month to order the seizure of the stake in oil producer \uc2 Bashneft\uc2  belonging to oil-to-telecoms conglomerate Sistema, and the placing of its oligarch boss under house arrest, has raised investors' fears that the Kremlin wants to reclaim prized assets.\par \par \uc2 Putin said he would not interfere in the legal case.\par \par \uc2 Asked about the conflict in Ukraine, where Russia denies sending troops and weapons to support pro-Russian separatists, he said the former Soviet republic was Russia's "closest, most brotherly nation" and hoped a parliamentary election on Oct. 26 would help bring stability to the country.\par \par \uc2 But overall, his message to those governments who imposed sanctions on Russia over its policy in Ukraine was clear - you will lose out. (Additional reporting by Alexei Anishchuk, Lidia Kelly, Jason Bush, Vladimir Soldatkin, Alessandra Prentice, Andrey Kuzmin, Anastasia Lyrchikova, Polina Devitt, Alexander Winning and Gabriela Baczynska; Writing by Elizabeth Piper; Editing by Timothy Heritage and Janet McBride) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-10-2T14:05:31.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020141002eaa2009ir\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscal