\par\par \b \uc2 *Russia Economy Minister: Economy Needs "Accurate" Monetary, Fiscal Stimulus\b0\par\par\uc2 408 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Oktober 2014\par 08:35\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 2 Oct 2014 02:36 ET *Russia Economy Minister: Russia Will Not Arrest Foreign Property in Response to Sanctions\par \par \uc2 2 Oct 2014 02:36 ET *Russia Economy Minister: Economic Problems Are Both Structural, Cyclical\par \par \uc2 2 Oct 2014 02:36 ET *Russia CB Chair: Economy Main Problem is Structural, Monetary Steps Alone Can't Help\par \par \uc2 2 Oct 2014 02:36 ET *Russia CB Head: No Capital Control is Achievement, Should be Preserved\par \par \uc2 2 Oct 2014 02:37 ET *Bank of Russia to Launch New \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  Repo Tool for Seven And 28 Days\par \par \uc2 2 Oct 2014 02:38 ET *\b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  CB Will Continue limited \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex Interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 -Chairwoman\par \par \uc2 2 Oct 2014 02:53 ET *\b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  Fin Min: Cabinet Unanimous On the Need For Structural Reforms\par \par \uc2 2 Oct 2014 02:53 ET *Russia Fin Min: Demand Stimulus Would Provide Only Temporary Gain, Should Be Avoided\par \par \uc2 2 Oct 2014 02:54 ET *Russia Fin Min Pledges To Continue Conservative Policy, Not Increase Budget Spending\par \par \uc2 2 Oct 2014 03:07 ET *Bank Of Russia Head: 2014 Inflation Seen At Around 8%, Above Target\par \par \uc2 2 Oct 2014 04:26 ET  Russian Officials Pledge Not to Control Capital Flows\par \par \uc2 By Alexander Kolyandr\par \par \uc2 MOSCOW--Russian government ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials Thursday pledged to continue a tight and conservative economic policy and not to attempt to stimulate demand or control capital flows.\par \par \uc2 Speaking at an investment conference, Russia's economy minister, finance minister and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's chairwoman said the country's economy is suffering from a cyclical downturn, geopolitical challenges and structural problems, of which the latter is the most important.\par \par \uc2 Russia's economy, suffering from waning investment, massive capital flight, high inflation and lack of access to the global capital markets as the result of sanctions, is stagnating and is expected to grow by only 0.4%-0.5% this year. Meanwhile, 2014 inflation is on track to reach 8%, overshooting the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's target of no more than 6.5%, said \uc2 Elvira Nabiullina\uc2 , chairwoman of the Bank of Russia.\par \par \uc2 Economy Minister Alexei Ulyukayev described the combination of low growth and high inflation as "flammable and explosive." He admitted that some fiscal and monetary stimuli are possible, but only "very accurate", "targeted" and "pinpointed", in order to avoid even higher inflation.\par \par \uc2 Write to Alexander Kolyandr at alexander.kolyandr@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 October 02, 2014 04:26 ET (08:26 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020141002eaa2000jr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
