\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. Will Watch Russia Closely Before Removing Sanctions -- WSJ Blog\b0\par\par\uc2 By William Mauldin   \par \uc2 409 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 September 2014\par 18:32\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 U.S. and European officials have said that the latest round of sanctions against Russia could be removed if the peace process outlined with Ukraine is completed.\par \par \uc2 But in Washington, the Obama administration made it quite clear that before any sanctions are removed, it wants to see the completion of all 12 points of the peace plan outlined in Minsk, Belarus, and the complete removal of Russian material and forces from Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 "We have yet to see real evidence that Russia has ceased its efforts to destabilize Ukraine," a senior administration official said on a call with reporters Friday.\par \par \uc2 Another senior administration official expressed hope that the round of sanctions unveiled Friday will carry through the entire peace process. Some EU member states had balked at the latest round of sanctions, delaying the implementation.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine is in talks to prevent a resumption of deadly fighting in its eastern regions bordering Russia. Officials in Kiev, Washington and Europe blame \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  for encouraging a separatist movement there and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  militarily, just months after it moved to annex Ukraine's Crimea region. Moscow says it hasn't sent forces into Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 The current sanctions are a response to what officials describe as major infusions of Russian troops, tanks and artillery into Ukraine to tip the battle against Kiev's forces.\par \par \uc2 The latest measures, which demand compliance this month, block Western energy majors from contributing technology, goods or services to Russian efforts to extract oil from the Arctic, deep offshore fields and shale formations. Financial services are excluded from the restrictions.\par \par \uc2 The sanctions are likely to affect the work of \uc2 Exxon Mobil\uc2  , which is working with Russian state-controlled oil giant OAO Rosneft in the Arctic Ocean.\par \par \uc2 Besides the energy sanctions, tougher financial sanctions from the U.S. and EU will restrict all but short-term financing to the biggest state-controlled banks that dominate Russia's financial sector, as well as some defense and industrial firms.\par \par \uc2 The Western sanctions are weighing on the Russian ruble and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's ability to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and U.S. officials expect the measures will continue to have a cumulative effect in the future.\par \par \uc2 More:\par \par \uc2 U.S., EU Widen Sanctions on Russia \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  More at The Wall Street Journal's Washington Wire blog, \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://blogs.wsj.com/washwire/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://blogs.wsj.com/washwire/}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 September 12, 2014 12:32 ET (16:32 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140912ea9c002pc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
