\par\par \b \uc2 Draghi Says Government Guarantees Would Make ABS Program More Effective -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,308 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 September 2014\par 16:36\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 1436 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ]--The ECB will expand its stimulus efforts to the riskier mezzanine tranches of asset-backed securities only if they are guaranteed by national governments, President \uc2 Mario Draghi\uc2  said Friday. For now, the purchases are oriented toward the senior, or least risky, tranches, Mr. Draghi said, but added that an inclusion of mezzanine tranches would make the program "much more effective." Germany and the Netherlands meanwhile said they were against government guarantees linked to the ABS program. (gabriele.steinhauser@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 1432 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ]--Eurozone finance ministers stopped short of calling for sanctions on France Friday, after Paris announced earlier this week that it would take an extra two years to cut its deficit to 3% of GDP, the maximum allowed under EU rules. However, by stressing the importance of the \uc2 European Commission\uc2 's review of France's 2015 budget this October, ministers indicated that Paris may well be asked to do more in terms of economic overhauls and spending cuts. (gabriele.steinhauser@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 10:27 EDT - Reuters/UMich consumer sentiment index for early September rises to 84.6, its highest since July 2013. But economists warn that's largely driven by the expectations index, which rose to a 15-month higher of 75.6. Pantheon Macroeconomics' \f28 \uc2 \f2 Ian Shepherdson\f28 \uc2 \f2  says that index is greatly influenced by moves in gasoline prices and the stock market, and that by historical standards, the 75-range remains weak. That subindex ranged in the 90s in early 2007. "Overall, encouraging, but it's not signalling a consumer boom, or even a boomlet," he says. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; @cynthialin_dj)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 10:24 EDT - \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Treasury\f28 \uc2 \f2  announces new sanctions against \f28 \uc2 \f2 Sberbank\f28 \uc2 \f2  of Russia, the country's largest bank, five defense technology firms and five companies engaged in exploration or production for Russian deepwater, Arctic offshore and shale projects. The latest steps by the U.S. and EU ratchet up pressure on Russia amid a ceasefire in Ukraine. "Given \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Russia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's direct military \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and blatant efforts to destabilize Ukraine, we have deepened our sanctions against Russia today," says Treasury Secretary \f28 \uc2 \f2 Jacob Lew\f28 \uc2 \f2 . But he also notes the latest penalties could be suspended if there's a lasting settlement to the conflict. (jeffrey.sparshott@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 10:23 EDT - The most powerful person in Peru isn't the president, an opposition politician, or even a banking or mining executive. It is First Lady Nadine Heredia, according to a new poll by Datum. The national poll of 1,209 people showed Heredia beating out her husband, President Ollanta Humala, when asked who has the most power in Peru. Heredia's influence in the government is well-known, but past polls have always shown that the president was considered holding more power. Heredia, currently the head of the ruling Nationalist party, is also seen as more influential than past president Alan Garcia, opposition leader Keiko Fujimori, and banking executive Dionisio Romero. (ryan.dube@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 10:08 EDT - Companies cautiously restocked in July amid a decent pickup in sales. Business inventories rose a seasonally adjusted 0.4% from June, while sales climbed 0.8%, the Commerce Department said. The ratio of firms' inventory to sales held steady and was slightly down from a year earlier. While inventories, relative to sales, haven't budged since February, they're generally running higher this year than earlier in the recession, a possible sign firms are gaining confidence in the strength of the recovery. (joshua.mitchell@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 09:51 EDT - A deluge of corporate-bond sales so far this month has weakened bond prices, but analysts at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barclays\f28 \uc2 \f2  say it has "not taken us low enough in the range to believe it is a market-wide buying opportunity." Investment-grade US bonds have returned negative 1.27% for the month, while high-yield bonds have returned a negative 1.02%. Still, the analysts say the downward move over the summer offers a better buying opportunity. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barclays\f28 \uc2 \f2  says they are not "overly negative on the asset class, but we are less inclined to position for a move that is too small to be considered a true dip." (mike.cherney@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 09:32 EDT - \f28 \uc2 \f2 Chile's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reduced its monetary policy rate to 3.25% late Thursday, while the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Central Reserve Bank of Peru\f28 \uc2 \f2  chopped its key lending rate to 3.50%, and more cuts may be in store, BBVA Research says. Chile's policy decision will depend on "short-term inflation figures; nonetheless we consider that further stimulus is likely to be necessary for the rest of the year." In Peru, "the tone of the statement accompanying yesterday's decision suggests that we cannot rule out a possible further cut in the policy rate before year-end," the bank says. (robert.kozak@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 9:28 EDT - \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of America Merrill Lynch\f28 \uc2 \f2  now sees the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fed\f28 \uc2 \f2  making its first rate increase in June 2015, up from its prior forecast for September 2015. The question now is when the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fed\f28 \uc2 \f2  will change its rhetoric enough to scare markets. Will it come in next Wednesday's FOMC statement? It's a close call, \f28 \uc2 \f2 BofAML\f28 \uc2 \f2  says. "They will likely try to change the statement in a market neutral fashion, dropping the reference to 'considerable time'." Firm also expects the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fed\f28 \uc2 \f2 's rate-forecast "dots" to shift slightly higher. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; @cynthialin_dj)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 09:22 EDT - Selling momentum sends the benchmark 10-year yield briefly above 2.6% for the first time in more than a month. The yield touches 2.601%, highest intraday level since the end of July, according to CQG. The bond market has sold off all week long amid concerns about the FOMC policy statement next week and if it'll signal a sooner rate hike. The 10-year Treasury note last down 18/32, yielding 2.598%. Still, the yield remains below 3%, where it started the year. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1259 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ]--Eurozone government bond issuance is scheduled from four countries next week. Slovakia taps the November 2018 and February 2023 bonds Tuesday with open volume, as usual. Germany follows Wednesday with the EUR4 billion reopening of the September 2016 Schatz. Spain launches a new three-year bond Thursday, maturing in October 2017, and on the same day France conducts its usual mid-month double feature. At its first auction, France offers EUR7-EUR8 billion November 2016, May 2019 and November 2019 conventional bonds, known as OATs. At the second auction it offers EUR1-EUR1.5 billion July 2018 and July 2024 OATei bonds [indexed to eurozone inflation] and July 2021 OATi [indexed to French inflation]. (emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 8:58 EDT - Some economists are asking why \f28 \uc2 \f2 Colombia's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  continues to buy dollars to weaken the peso when the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has already fallen 7% in seven weeks, to 1981/USD? "The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  should soon reassess their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy," Jefferies says in a research note. Bank officials have their reasons; an even weaker peso would further help Colombian exporters, and keeping up with \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will build \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves against shocks. But Jefferies says "this strategy could be self-defeating if it feeds into a vicious cycle of de-leveraging and then need for more USD as well as the pressure on the inflation target." (dan.molinski@wsj.com @dmwsj)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 September 12, 2014 10:36 ET (14:36 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140912ea9c00293\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
