\par\par \b \uc2 US Hits Russia's Biggest Bank, Others with Sanctions -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,313 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 September 2014\par 16:24\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 10:24 EDT - \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  announces new sanctions against \uc2 Sberbank\uc2  of Russia, the country's largest bank, five defense technology firms and five companies engaged in exploration or production for Russian deepwater, Arctic offshore and shale projects. The latest steps by the U.S. and EU ratchet up pressure on Russia amid a ceasefire in Ukraine. "Given \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's direct military \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and blatant efforts to destabilize Ukraine, we have deepened our sanctions against Russia today," says Treasury Secretary \uc2 Jacob Lew\uc2 . But he also notes the latest penalties could be suspended if there's a lasting settlement to the conflict. (jeffrey.sparshott@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 10:23 EDT - Massachusetts is planning to sell another $350M in so-called "green bonds" next week, meaning proceeds from the bond sale will be used for environmentally friendly projects. The deal comes after the state sold $100M in such bonds last year, in a deal that was billed as the first of its kind in the municipal bond market. The state is tapping into growing investor thirst for green investments. "Last year's demand for green bonds was overwhelming," state Treasurer Steven Grossman says. Companies in recent years have also stepped up their issuance of green bonds. (mike.cherney@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 10:18 EDT - August saw $41B in buyback authorizations, a decline of 11% from August of 2013, according to Birinyi Associates. The recent slowdown in buyback authorizations are probably a sign that share repurchases will slow down in the latter part of the year after 1Q's multiyear record. On the other hand, the number of companies buying back stock is the highest it's been since 2008, at 740. Birinyi says announced buybacks should total $638B this year, the fourth highest of all time. (dan.strumpf@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 10:08 EDT - Companies cautiously restocked in July amid a decent pickup in sales. Business inventories rose a seasonally adjusted 0.4% from June, while sales climbed 0.8%, the Commerce Department said. The ratio of firms' inventory to sales held steady and was slightly down from a year earlier. While inventories, relative to sales, haven't budged since February, they're generally running higher this year than earlier in the recession, a possible sign firms are gaining confidence in the strength of the recovery. (joshua.mitchell@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 9:58 EDT - U.S. auto sales could be headed back to earth following a blockbuster August. \uc2 Wells Fargo\uc2  says its September survey indicates the seasonally adjusted annualized rate of sales, or SAAR, will fall in the 16.3M to 16.4M range, compared with 17.4M in August. While lower on a sequential basis, the rate would be far higher than the 15.3M SAAR in September 2013. If \uc2 Wells Fargo\uc2 's forecast is accurate, the average light vehicle SAAR for the first nine months would be 16.3M, the strongest pace in several years. (john.stoll@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 09:51 EDT - A deluge of corporate-bond sales so far this month has weakened bond prices, but analysts at \uc2 Barclays\uc2  say it has "not taken us low enough in the range to believe it is a market-wide buying opportunity." Investment-grade US bonds have returned negative 1.27% for the month, while high-yield bonds have returned a negative 1.02%. Still, the analysts say the downward move over the summer offers a better buying opportunity. \uc2 Barclays\uc2  says they are not "overly negative on the asset class, but we are less inclined to position for a move that is too small to be considered a true dip." (mike.cherney@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 9:49 EDT - Many on Wall Street continue to question the rationale of \uc2 AT&T\uc2 's (T) $49B deal for \uc2 DirecTV\uc2  (DTV) and T continues to make the case. At a \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  conference Friday, \uc2 AT&T\uc2 's Ralph de a Vega highlighted plans to offer a combined satellite dish and wireless antenna so rural customers can get both services from one service visit. Also, the company sees opportunity in using \uc2 DirecTV\uc2  to bring content to the back seat of cars through deal with companies like \uc2 GM\uc2 . Meanwhile, DTV CEO says his "best guess" is that the deal will close in early April. (thomas.gryta@wsj.com; @tgryta)\par \par \uc2 9:45 EDT - After being in the doghouse for a string of quarters now, fixed-income, \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and commodities trading is attracting less dire predictions from analysts. After meeting with \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2 , (\uc2 JPM\uc2 ) Oppenheimer analysts -- who've previously noted that big banks' FICC businesses face greater secular challenges -- say they're "more optimistic that there is also a large component of cyclical factors contributing to the current slack trading environment." Separately, after meeting with \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  (GS), \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  says higher volatility caused by the "decoupling of global macro-economic and monetary policies" could drive increased activity, although they note "trends remain episodic." (Saabira.Chaudhuri@wsj.com, @SaabiraC)\par \par \uc2 09:42 EDT - Stocks seem "oblivious" to the march to war against ISIS, writes Raymond James' \uc2 Jeffrey Saut\uc2 . The \uc2 Dow\uc2  barely budged yesterday in the wake of President Obama's speech outlining plans to attack the terror group, despite a cool reception by Middle East allies. "What are we going to do if much of the western world decides not to defeat ISIS?" he asks. It's the latest instance this year of the stock market largely shrugging off turmoil overseas. Similarly, \uc2 Kent Engelke\uc2  of \uc2 Capitol Securities\uc2  says there was "little reaction to the president's speech." Stocks edge lower just after the opening bell Friday. (dan.strumpf@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 9:40 EDT - Wedbush decides not to wait for next week's blockbuster IPO to take place before it initiates shares of Alibaba (BABA) at outperform with an $80 price target. Firm says China's secular e-commerce trends are driving high growth rates across Alibaba's platforms, which it says can produce gross merchandise volumes greater than Amazon (AMZN) and \uc2 EBAY\uc2  combined. Wedbush says company provides "the most compelling combinations of size, growth and profitability, on a global basis." (patrick.sullivan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 9:40 EDT - Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and stocks drop after the most recent poll shows incumbent President \uc2 Dilma Rousseff\uc2  gained ground ahead of October's presidential race. According to Ibope poll, candidate Marina Silva maintained a small lead over Rousseff, but the incumbent is closing the gap. Ms. Silva leads Ms. Rousseff 43% to 42% in the second round of voting, vs 47%-43% in the previous poll. Brazil's Ibovespa stock index down 1.6%, while the real trades at 2.321 per dollar vs 2.2901 yesterday. Ms. Rousseff's reelection is considered negative by investors who perceive her as promoting \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies in key sectors of the economy. (rogerio.jelmayer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 09:32 EDT - \uc2 Chile's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reduced its monetary policy rate to 3.25% late Thursday, while the \uc2 Central Reserve Bank of Peru\uc2  chopped its key lending rate to 3.50%, and more cuts may be in store, BBVA Research says. Chile's policy decision will depend on "short-term inflation figures; nonetheless we consider that further stimulus is likely to be necessary for the rest of the year." In Peru, "the tone of the statement accompanying yesterday's decision suggests that we cannot rule out a possible further cut in the policy rate before year-end," the bank says. (robert.kozak@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par To contact the Market Talk Editors, \par Kevin Kingsbury, 212 416-2354, kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com \par Cynthia Lin, 212 416-4403, Cynthia.lin@wsj.com \par John Shipman, 212 416-2181, john.shipman@wsj.com \par Patrick Sullivan, 212 416-2326, Patrick.sullivan@wsj.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 September 12, 2014 10:24 ET (14:24 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140912ea9c0023t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
