\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-Rouble weakens after Ukraine says destroys Russian armoured column\b0\par\par\uc2 378 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 August 2014\par 17:54\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2014 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 (Recasts after Poroshenko comments)\par \par \uc2 MOSCOW, Aug 15 (Reuters) - The rouble weakened on Friday after Ukraine said its artillery had attacked and destroyed a "significant" part of a Russian armoured column that crossed into Ukrainian territory overnight.\par \par \uc2 By 1540 GMT, the rouble was 0.41 percent weaker against the dollar at 36.18 since Friday's market opening and had lost 1.02 percent to trade at 48.55 versus the euro. .\par \par \uc2 The Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had strengthened against the dollar earlier in the session but dropped sharply after reports of the incident were published, leaving it 0.51 percent weaker at 41.70 against the dollar-euro basket the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  uses to gauge the rouble's nominal exchange rate.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine's President Petro Poroshenko said in a statement on his website that Ukrainian forces had attacked the Russian column overnight.\par \par \uc2 A Ukrainian military spokesman separately told journalists that Ukrainian forces had tracked the column as soon as it crossed onto Ukrainian soil. Russia denies sending troops into Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 The Ukrainian comments hit Russian shares in late trading.\par \par \uc2 Russia's dollar-denominated RTS index ended 0.03 percent higher from Friday's opening at 1,232 points, while the rouble-based MICEX index traded 0.7 percent higher on the day at 1,417 points.\par \par \uc2 Both indexes had risen more than 4 percent over the week on an apparent easing of tensions between Moscow and the West, but investors had remained wary after repeated false dawns.\par \par \uc2 "Geopolitics is still the main driver for the Russian stock market," said Mikhail Kuzmin of Moscow-based analytical firm Investcafe.\par \par \uc2 The United States and \uc2 European Union\uc2  have threatened tougher sanctions if \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  directly in eastern Ukraine, where government troops are closing in on rebel-held areas.\par \par \uc2 Further sanctions, which have so far targeted Russian businessmen and companies as well as the banking, energy and defence sectors, would hurt Russian asset prices by scaring away investors and dampening the outlook for Russia's economy.\par \par \uc2 For rouble poll data see\par \par \uc2 For Russian equities guide see\par \par \uc2 For Russian treasury bonds see\par \par \uc2 Russia in graphics: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://link.reuters.com/dun63s"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://link.reuters.com/dun63s}}}\uc2  (Reporting by Alexander Winning; Editing by Ruth Pitchford) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-8-15T16:54:28.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020140815ea8f00527\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
