\par\par \b \uc2 European Morning Briefing: Stocks Seen Slightly Lower\b0\par\par\uc2 3,468 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 August 2014\par 06:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par SNAPSHOT: \par ======== \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European stock markets seen slightly lower, bunds and gilts mixed; euro, spot gold, oil lower\f28 \par \par \uc2 -Obama Warns \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  Against Further \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Ukraine\par \par \uc2 -U.K. BRC Jul Total Retail Sales +1.3% On Year\par \par \uc2 -Gross Cuts U.S. Government-Related Debt at \uc2 Pimco Total Return Fund\par \par \uc2 -Actor, Comedian Robin Williams Dead In 'Apparent Suicide'\par \par \uc2 -\uc2 J.P. Morgan\uc2  Agrees to Sell Roughly Half Its Investment in \uc2 One Equity Partners\par \par \uc2 -\uc2 UniCredit\uc2  Says \uc2 Bank Austria\uc2  Dispute Could Result in More Payouts\par \par \uc2 WATCH FOR: Germany ZEW current economic conditions data; U.S. small business optimism, API weekly oil data \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EUROPEAN OUTLOOK & U.S./ASIAN SUMMARIES: \par ======== \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors are taking a more relaxed view of regional trouble spots, and European stock markets are likely to drift slightly lower to start after Wall Street closed off its highs, with gilts and bunds mixed amid little demand for additional safety at this juncture.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Asian stocks traded mixed on Tuesday, as easing geopolitical concerns allowed Japan to extend its recovery from sharp falls at the end of last week.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With concerns over Iraq and Ukraine subsiding, traders in Tokyo were able to focus on factors that have supported stocks in recent weeks.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Corporate earnings reports have been good overall, stock valuations remain cheap, and hopes for more government pension buying in domestic equities are solid," said Hiroichi Nishi, general manager of equities at SMBC Nikko Securities.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Wall Street futures were higher, with Treasurys lower, as the euro, spot gold and oil futures eased lower. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par STOCKS: \par ====== \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors have dissected the latest developments in the troubles in Gaza, Iraq and Ukraine to conclude that for now, little impact on the global economic picture seems likely.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Ahead of Tuesday's opening, IG has called the FTSE flat at 6633, the DAX off 9 at 9172, and the CAC down 7 at 4191.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Markets continue to differentiate between the humanitarian crises in Gaza and Iraq, which are not market drivers, and the Ukraine conflict, which has far more potential to spill over to asset prices. The lack of news on the last of these supports the risk bounce," said Kit Juckes, an analyst at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Soci\u233\'00\'E9t\u233\'00\'E9 G\u233\'00\'E9n\u233\'00\'E9rale\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Evan Lucas, strategist at IG, said a further sign of stabilization came as "Russia has taken a further step back from the Ukrainian crisis, after having pulled back strike fighters from the border. However, Russian troops still remain."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Wall Street futures were higher Tuesday, after stocks ended slightly higher Monday, paring earlier gains, with investors buoyed by improving economic data and shrugging off tensions overseas.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Domestically, second-quarter earnings and economic data have shown improvement.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The underlying economic trends have been great...earnings season has been great," said Jerry Braakman, chief investment officer of \f28 \uc2 \f2 First American Trust\f28 \uc2 \f2 , which manages $1.1 billion. He said the tensions overseas that have grabbed headlines in recent months "are more external" and have little impact on the fundamentals of the U.S. stock market. \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \par ====== \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro fell against the dollar in Asia as investors used the easing of geopolitical tensions to make bets on weaker euro-zone growth and inflation in data due later in the week.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors predict growth in the second quarter in the euro zone overall, as well as in Germany and France, will be lackluster, while inflation in the euro bloc for July will stay at June's level of 0.4%, well short of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's target of 2%. On Thursday, numbers for second-quarter gross domestic product will be released for the euro zone, Germany and France; the euro zone will also report consumer-price index numbers for July.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors predict the weak economic numbers will eventually prompt the ECB to launch a broad asset-purchase program, similar to those used by central banks in Japan, the U.K. and the U.S., which would weaken the single \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par BONDS: \par ===== \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bunds and gilts may start mixed with the German ZEW indicator of economic conditions due out.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Economic conditions in Germany appear to be deteriorating, in part because of fallout from sanctions against Russia and waning confidence related to the conflict in the Ukraine. So it would hardly surprise anyone to see the ZEW measure of business confidence fall. A loss of optimism within the euro zone's biggest economy would complicate the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's fight against deflationary forces.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Even so, bund yields remain just above Friday's all-time low of 1.02%, with tensions in markets still simmering.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The situation [in Ukraine] is likely to remain both fluid and tense as the Ukrainian army approaches the rebel-held city of Donetsk," said interest-rate strategists at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Rabobank\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "From a fixed-income perspective, expectations are that we'll see less money go into the euro zone and more into the U.S. because the data is better in the U.S.," said Sebastien Galy, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Societe Generale\f28 \uc2 \f2 , and that is weighing on the euro.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. Treasury\f28 \uc2 \f2  bonds were slightly lower Tuesday, after they clawed back price losses on Monday, shaking off higher global stocks and looming new debt issuance.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Buyers stepped into the haven bond market after an earlier round of selling, a sign bond investors remain cautious over the geopolitical risks in Ukraine and Middle East.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "There are buyers on dip until the global hot spots, notably Ukraine, cool," said Mary Ann Hurley, vice president of trading in Seattle at \f28 \uc2 \f2 D.A. Davidson & Co\f28 \uc2 \f2 . "Bond investors wait for a clearer picture."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Analysts reckon the geopolitical tensions in Ukraine and the Middle East will spare the pace of global economic growth. But they caution that any signs of deterioration could jolt investor sentiment again and they could sell stocks and start buying safer \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. government\f28 \uc2 \f2  debt again.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "There is a certain amount of money coming into the Treasury market from sources other than stocks, and some of it is coming from high-yield bonds," said Kevin Giddis, head of fixed income at Raymond James. "There is a nervousness about holding risk that doesn't pay very much to those that take it."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In Japan Tuesday, government bonds slightly lower. Shuichi Ohsaki, a bond strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of America Merrill Lynch\f28 \uc2 \f2 , said falls in JGB prices were mostly due to position adjustments, adding that there remains enough demand to keep the gradual uptrend intact. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par ENERGY: \par ======= \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Oil futures were slightly lower in Asia as traders continue to monitor the world's hot spots for signs of potential disruption to supplies.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The market appeared to be stabilizing after a slide that has knocked about 8% off barrel prices in the U.S. and 9% globally since mid-June highs, with the U.S. benchmark touching a six-month low last week, said Gene McGillian, a broker at wholesale brokerage Tradition Energy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Still, with limited other fundamental drivers, geopolitics and other external factors like equity moves could continue to play an outsized role in the crude-oil markets, Ritterbusch & Associates said. "We look for geopolitical developments and equity swings to generally steer oil pricing through the rest of this week," the firm said in a note.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Nymex crude was down 19 cents at $97.89, while Brent was off 13 cents at $104.55. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par METALS: \par ====== \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Spot gold fell $1.40 to $1,307.08 in Asia, as traders shrug off trouble spots of Gaza, Ukraine, Libya and Iraq for now.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Gold's reaction to the news has been lackluster, reflecting a market that is unconvinced that these smoldering conflicts can catch fire, said Adam Klopfenstein, a senior commodities strategist with Archer Financial Services in Chicago.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The geopolitical uncertainty is keeping gold from falling down," he said, adding that gold has failed to move substantially higher.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 LME 3-month copper was up $5.25 at $7,005 as traders assessed chances for China to stimulate the economy and therefore demand, after inflation registered only 2.3% year-to-year in July.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Write to Dennis Baker at dennis.baker@wsj.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 12 Aug 2014 00:00 ET \par European Morning Briefing: DJ Dominant Headlines; Other Top Stories\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 DJ Dominant Headlines:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Asian Exchanges Eye Hong Kong-Shanghai Trading Link\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Acciona, \f28 \uc2 \f2 KKR\f28 \uc2 \f2  Hire Banks for IPO of Renewable-Energy Assets\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Disney\f28 \uc2 \f2 -Amazon Dispute Concerns More Than Pricing\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Vascular Biogenics\f28 \uc2 \f2  Says It Didn't Go Public After All\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Other Top Stories:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Obama Warns \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Russia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Against Further \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in Ukraine\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 President Barack Obama on Monday warned against further Russian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in Ukraine, in his latest move to discourage Moscow from sending troops to prop up separatists under the guise of humanitarian relief.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Allergan\f28 \uc2 \f2 , Valeant Get Second Information Request From FTC\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Allergan Inc\f28 \uc2 \f2 . and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Valeant Pharmaceuticals Inc\f28 \uc2 \f2 . said they received a second request from the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Trade Commission\f28 \uc2 \f2  seeking information about Valeant and investor William Ackman's takeover bid.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Chiquita Gets $625 Million Buyout Offer From Cutrale, Safra\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A pair of Brazilian companies unleashed a $625 million offer Monday for \f28 \uc2 \f2 Chiquita Brands International Inc\f28 \uc2 \f2 ., seeking to thwart the U.S. banana company's plans to merge with Ireland's Fyffes.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Wanda Commercial Properties to Invest $271M in Gold Coast Project\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Chinese conglomerate Wanda Commercial Properties (Group) Co. is investing up to 2.1 billion Hong Kong dollars ($271 million) in a project in Gold Coast, Australia, marking its latest venture abroad and its first investment Down Under.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Nominee to Replace Maliki as Iraqi Premier Is Seen as Less Divisive\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 If Nouri al-Maliki, the embattled prime minister of Iraq, was considered by many Iraqis and Western officials as sectarian and parochial, the man who might succeed him, Haider al-Abadi, appears in many ways the opposite.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 12 Aug 2014 00:00 ET \par European Morning Briefing: Market Prices and G7 Calendar\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par DJIA    16569.98  +16.05 +0.10% \par Nasdaq   4401.33  +30.43 +0.70% \par S&P 500  1936.92   +5.33 +0.28% \par FTSE 100 6632.82  +65.46 +1.00% \par DAX      9180.74 +171.42 +1.90% \par CAC40    4197.70  +49.89 +1.20% \par Closing \par  \par Futures: \par Dow    16540.00 +25.00 +0.2% \par NASDAQ   3911.25 +6.25 +0.2% \par S&P      1935.40 +2.80 +0.1% \par As of 0330 GMT \par  \par Nikkei    15178.77  +48.25 +0.32% \par Hang Seng 24596.49  -49.53 -0.20% \par S&P/ASX    5511.50  +54.50 +1.00% \par Mumbai    25519.24 +190.10 +0.75% \par Kospi      2043.34   +3.97 +0.19% \par Shanghai   2215.42   -9.23 -0.42% \par Taipei     9162.82  -10.09 -0.11% \par As of 0325 GMT \par  \par USD/JPY 102.29-32 +0.11% \par Range   102.36 102.18 \par EUR/USD 1.3369-72 -0.11% \par Range   1.3387 1.3368 \par GBP/USD 1.6770-74 -0.09% \par Range   1.6789 1.6771 \par USD/CHF 0.9077-82 +0.15% \par Range   0.9082 0.9066 \par AUD/USD 0.9258-62 -0.03% \par Range   0.9268 0.9250 \par EUR/JPY 136.78-82 +0.01% \par Range   136.88 136.76 \par EUR/GBP 0.7971-73 -0.02% \par Range   0.7978 0.7970 \par EUR/CHF 1.2138-42 +0.04% \par Range   1.2141 1.2132 \par As of 0250 GMT vs. NY close \par  \par  \par USD/JPY Vol Option 5.225%/5.525% \par Day Earlier: 5.550%/5.950% \par EUR/USD Vol Option 4.700%/4.950% \par Day Earlier: 4.713%/5.012% \par AUD/USD Vol Option 6.00