\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. Dollar Gathers a Little Strength -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,381 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Juli 2014\par 14:56\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 8:55 EDT - US dollar appears a bit firmer, but U.S. economic data haven't done any of the heavy lifting in its behalf. The yen weakened against rivals after Japan's jobless rate ticked up in June, while retail sales for the month fell below forecasts. Euro slipped after the US and EU agreed on, but didn't announce, sanctions against Russia, which are expected to ultimately hurt euro-zone exports and affect energy imports. Also, euro-zone bond yields are falling faster than those of US Treasurys. The greenback climbed 0.2% vs the yen, to (Yen) 102.07, highest since July 7. Euro lost 0.2% against the dollar, to $1.3416, its low since Nov. 21, 2013. (james.ramage@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 8:42 EDT - As the 10-year Treasury yield approached 2014's low the past week, sentiment turned the most bearish on bonds in 8 years. Investors who expect bond yields to rise climbed to 42% for the week ended Monday from 40% a week ago, according to the weekly Treasury client survey by \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2 . Those expecting yields to fall ticked down to 13% from 15%. Bears have lost ground on their short wagers this year, but many folks are still expecting the 10-year yield to rise to 3% or higher by year's end. Further yield declines will likely spurs the pain trade among bears as they need to cover. (min.zeng@wsj.com;@minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 8:33 EDT - Rise in Brent oil prices recently sparked by geopolitical tensions hasn't translated into broader support for Western Canada Select, with the spread between the two prices at a four-month high, Scotiabank says. This is negative for Canada's terms of trade and the loonie, it adds. (nirmala.menon@wsj.com, @NirmalaMenon)\par \par \uc2 1216 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Poland's economy will continue accelerating next year, with growth reaching 4.5% annually, says PKO BP chief economist Radoslaw Bodys, adding higher-than-expected growth will be driven by fast growth in private consumption and broad infrastructure investments tied with EU funds. \uc2 Poland's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  believes growth will be stable at 3.6% in 2015. Mr. Bodys also bets the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will keep interest rates unchanged at 2.5% at least for next 12 months despite temporary deflation over summer months. (patryk.wasilewski@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1215 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] European Summary: With the EU/U.S. agreeing late Monday to adopt new sanctions against Russia, the ruble tumbled to it lowest level vs EUR and USD since early May, while haven flows into German bunds sent the yield on the 10-year to a record low. Equity markets showed less impact with Moscow's Micex just 0.2% lower, while the main European bourses added around 0.5%, helped by some decent 1H earnings results. Elsewhere the USD retains a general safety bid, while sterling fell to a fresh 6-week low of $1.6950 despite upbeat June mortgage data. (gary.stride@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 8:07 EDT - The rally in bunds that sent the 10-year yield to record low propped up prices of Treasury bonds. Lower bund yield vs comparable Treasurys has been one of the main factors boosting \f28 \uc2 \f2 US government\f28 \uc2 \f2  bond prices this year. Tuesday, the 10-year bund yield fell to as low as 1.12%. The prospect of more monetary stimulus from ECB amid tame inflation has sent euro zone yields tumbling. Renewed jitters over the health of the big Portuguese bank also boosted haven bond demand, though the 10-year Portugal bond yield rose only by 2.6bps to 3.615%, a sign fears are contained. A sale of $35B in five-year notes is due at 1 p.m. The 10-year note is 4/32 higher, yielding 2.474%. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1137 GMT -- Market sentiment towards the ruble continues to deteriorate as the risk of sanctions on Russia increase says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Morgan Stanley\f28 \uc2 \f2 . The bank holds a negative view on the ruble, particularly as the RUB basket is still around 2.8% away from the level where the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Russia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will start to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . It adds that the longer tensions continue, the more likely other CEE regions will be affected. For now \f28 \uc2 \f2 Morgan Stanley\f28 \uc2 \f2  remains constructive toward both PLN and HUF over the medium-term but the possibility of a growth downturn related to downside growth risks to Russia could prompt near-term PLN and HUF weakness. USD/RUB trades at a fresh 12-week high of 35.746. USD/HUF 231.27, USD/PLN 3.0919, both highs for the USD from early February. (gary.stride@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 7:30 EDT - Following early weakness, US stock-index futures have moved solidly in the green amid gains in Europe and some early upbeat earnings news from the US healthcare sector. Those results come ahead of some 2nd-tier data later this morning, but those economic numbers pale in comparison to what's coming the rest of the week, starting tomorrow with the ADP jobs report and the FOMC statement. S&P 500 futures are up 3 while gold climbs further and oil is little changed. The dollar is a touch higher, with the euro at $1.3433, and Treasurys are up too. The 10-year yield is at 2.48%. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com; @kevinkingsbury)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1119 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ]--The official Xinhua news agency says China has arrested former security chief Zhou Yongkang for "serious disciplinary violation," a standard euphemism for corruption. Mr. Zhou is the biggest tiger yet to be snared in President Xi Jinping's anti-corruption drive - the question for markets now is whether this marks the high-water mark of the campaign, or just the next stage. "The demand shock in the short term as a consequence of the campaign cannot be ignored," said BAML economist Ting Lu in a note last month. Mr. Lu puts the impact at 1% of GDP. (richard.silk@dowjones.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 7:10 EDT - Stocks markets have continued to rally in Asia, despite somewhat lackluster trading on Wall Street. There seems to be a revival of speculative fervor, as investors bet that recent gains in Chinese economic data point to a recovery. Stocks in Shanghai post their sixth-straight gains, Japan stocks hit six-month highs on the back of improved earnings and Aussie shares post another six-year high. What's also compelling is that it occurs right before the FOMC's policy meeting. Clearly, no one's worried about the Fed signaling a near-term interest rate hike. But the idea of an actual earlier-than-expected rate hike seems to us like the precisely the kind of thing that could test the viability of this new round of Asian speculation. Remember the taper tantrum. (michael.casey@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1108 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ]--Sterling falls to a fresh 6-week low of $1.6951 Tuesday, amid generalized dollar strength. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Commerzbank\f28 \uc2 \f2  technical analyst Karen Jones is now looking for a test of the 2014 uptrend-line at $1.6905, with a break below here targeting sub $1.67 levels. Now at $1.6955, Ms. Jones says any intraday rally should run out of steam ahead of $1.7035. The peak so far Tuesday has been $1.6994. (gary.stride@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 6:56 EDT - Brazil could see \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  outflows soon as investors likely assume a more-defensive posture in the run-up to October's presidential election, says Sidnei Nehme at Sao Paulo's NGO brokerage. He added flows could further be hurt by companies in Brazil having already tapped international debt markets. (rogerio.jelmayer@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 July 29, 2014 08:56 ET (12:56 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140729ea7t001ra\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
