\par\par \b \uc2 European Summary Ruble Weighed By Sanctions Threat - -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,416 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Juli 2014\par 14:15\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 1215 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] European Summary: With the EU/U.S. agreeing late Monday to adopt new sanctions against Russia, the ruble tumbled to it lowest level vs EUR and USD since early May, while haven flows into German bunds sent the yield on the 10-year to a record low. Equity markets showed less impact with Moscow's Micex just 0.2% lower, while the main European bourses added around 0.5%, helped by some decent 1H earnings results. Elsewhere the USD retains a general safety bid, while sterling fell to a fresh 6-week low of $1.6950 despite upbeat June mortgage data. (gary.stride@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 8:07 EDT - The rally in bunds that sent the 10-year yield to record low propped up prices of Treasury bonds. Lower bund yield vs comparable Treasurys has been one of the main factors boosting \uc2 US government\uc2  bond prices this year. Tuesday, the 10-year bund yield fell to as low as 1.12%. The prospect of more monetary stimulus from ECB amid tame inflation has sent euro zone yields tumbling. Renewed jitters over the health of the big Portuguese bank also boosted haven bond demand, though the 10-year Portugal bond yield rose only by 2.6bps to 3.615%, a sign fears are contained. A sale of $35B in five-year notes is due at 1 p.m. The 10-year note is 4/32 higher, yielding 2.474%. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 1137 GMT -- Market sentiment towards the ruble continues to deteriorate as the risk of sanctions on Russia increase says \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2 . The bank holds a negative view on the ruble, particularly as the RUB basket is still around 2.8% away from the level where the \b \highlight22\uc2 central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  will start to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It adds that the longer tensions continue, the more likely other CEE regions will be affected. For now \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  remains constructive toward both PLN and HUF over the medium-term but the possibility of a growth downturn related to downside growth risks to Russia could prompt near-term PLN and HUF weakness. USD/RUB trades at a fresh 12-week high of 35.746. USD/HUF 231.27, USD/PLN 3.0919, both highs for the USD from early February. (gary.stride@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 7:30 EDT - Following early weakness, US stock-index futures have moved solidly in the green amid gains in Europe and some early upbeat earnings news from the US healthcare sector. Those results come ahead of some 2nd-tier data later this morning, but those economic numbers pale in comparison to what's coming the rest of the week, starting tomorrow with the ADP jobs report and the FOMC statement. S&P 500 futures are up 3 while gold climbs further and oil is little changed. The dollar is a touch higher, with the euro at $1.3433, and Treasurys are up too. The 10-year yield is at 2.48%. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com; @kevinkingsbury)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1119 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ]--The official Xinhua news agency says China has arrested former security chief Zhou Yongkang for "serious disciplinary violation," a standard euphemism for corruption. Mr. Zhou is the biggest tiger yet to be snared in President Xi Jinping's anti-corruption drive - the question for markets now is whether this marks the high-water mark of the campaign, or just the next stage. "The demand shock in the short term as a consequence of the campaign cannot be ignored," said BAML economist Ting Lu in a note last month. Mr. Lu puts the impact at 1% of GDP. (richard.silk@dowjones.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 7:10 EDT - Stocks markets have continued to rally in Asia, despite somewhat lackluster trading on Wall Street. There seems to be a revival of speculative fervor, as investors bet that recent gains in Chinese economic data point to a recovery. Stocks in Shanghai post their sixth-straight gains, Japan stocks hit six-month highs on the back of improved earnings and Aussie shares post another six-year high. What's also compelling is that it occurs right before the FOMC's policy meeting. Clearly, no one's worried about the Fed signaling a near-term interest rate hike. But the idea of an actual earlier-than-expected rate hike seems to us like the precisely the kind of thing that could test the viability of this new round of Asian speculation. Remember the taper tantrum. (michael.casey@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1108 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ]--Sterling falls to a fresh 6-week low of $1.6951 Tuesday, amid generalized dollar strength. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Commerzbank\f28 \uc2 \f2  technical analyst Karen Jones is now looking for a test of the 2014 uptrend-line at $1.6905, with a break below here targeting sub $1.67 levels. Now at $1.6955, Ms. Jones says any intraday rally should run out of steam ahead of $1.7035. The peak so far Tuesday has been $1.6994. (gary.stride@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 6:56 EDT - Brazil could see \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  outflows soon as investors likely assume a more-defensive posture in the run-up to October's presidential election, says Sidnei Nehme at Sao Paulo's NGO brokerage. He added flows could further be hurt by companies in Brazil having already tapped international debt markets. (rogerio.jelmayer@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1054 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ]--Poland's bond yields are likely to rise slightly in coming months following a recent rally that brought the yield on 10-year paper to around 3.22% due to local rate cut expectations, says PKO BP's chief market strategist Mariusz Adamiak. This yield is expected to rise to 3.5% at the end of the third quarter mainly driven by expectations of tighter monetary policy in the U.S. and resulting rise in global yields. Mr. Adamiak doesn't expect U.S. tapering to result in a significant sell-off of Poland's treasurys, but rather a gradual rise in the yield towards 4% in 2015. (patryk.wasilewski@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 6:44 EDT - Total credit in the Brazilian economy is likely to show signs of recovery in June data, with gains from consumer and corporate loans, says the National Federation of Banks. It predicts total credit rose 1.5% from May to BRL2.85T ($1.28T), versus a 1% gain that month. Amid economic weakness and increased indebtedness, Brazilians' demand for credit weakened to start 2014. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will release June's figures later this morning. (rogerio.jelmayer@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1043 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ]--The ruble is under pressure again, with the euro climbing above 48 rubles for the first time since May 13. This comes as a new round of tougher sanctions from the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Union\f28 \uc2 \f2  aimed at targeting specific sectors of Russian economy are expected to be announced as early Tuesday. The EU--Russia's largest trading partner--is expected to restrict transactions with Russia's state banks, as well as limit technology exports vital for the country's oil and weapons industries. The U.S. has vowed to follow suit. (olga.razumovskaya@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1037 GMT --\f28 \uc2 \f2 Alastair Thomas\f28 \uc2 \f2 , head of rates and treasury management at \f28 \uc2 \f2 ECM Asset Management\f28 \uc2 \f2 , says that the risk of Russia sanctions triggering "a blow-back into the European economy" is driving the rally in German Bunds. "If sanctions are particularly tough, they could pose a risk to growth in the region. That could provide more impetus for the ECB to engage in quantitative easing later in the year," he says. He adds that data indicate the European recovery is still relatively fragile and there is a risk of it being de-railed. German 10-year Bund yields are at an all-time low of 1.122%. (josie.cox@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 July 29, 2014 08:15 ET (12:15 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140729ea7t001pf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
