\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL MARKETS-European shares head for first weekly drop since April\b0\par\par\uc2 792 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Juni 2014\par 15:19\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2014 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * European shares inch up, but Iraq, data weigh\par     * Most Asian markets down with U.S. outlook in spotlight\par     * U.S. 10-year yield near 3-week low, German yield near\par 1-year trough\par     * Canadian dollar at 6-month high following inflation\par surprise\par     * Wall Street set for second week of losses in three\par \par     By Marc Jones\par     LONDON, June 27 (Reuters) - Concern about Iraq and Ukraine\par and subdued economic data left European shares facing their\par first week of losses since early April on Friday. Gold rose to\par near a two-month high.\par     Other safe-haven assets - the yen, Swiss franc and German\par and \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. government\f28 \uc2 \f2  bonds - were also in demand as investors\par backed away from the riskier bets they have been making for much\par of this year.\par     Stock rose slightly in London, Frankfurt \par and Paris but fell in Greece and Portugal. Losses\par earlier in the week meant European shares were set to bring a\par 10-week run of gains to an end. \par     Fighting between Iraqi forces and insurgents raged in the\par home town the late dictator Saddam Hussein and Russia warned of\par "grave consequences" as Ukraine signed a trade and political\par agreement with the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Union\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\par   \par     The uncertainty, along with a run of disappointing data this\par week, meant that Wall Street was poised for another\par subdued start and its second week of losses in the last\par three.\par     "Part of it was the GDP shock two days ago, then we get the\par soft consumer spending data," said Philip Marey, a U.S. focused\par economist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Rabobank\f28 \uc2 \f2 . "A few weeks ago, there was this big\par exuberance and the sky was the limit and now those hopes have\par really faded."\par     Gold was closing in on a fourth straight weekly gain at\par $1,315 an ounce, as the geopolitical unrest boosted its appeal\par and the soft U.S. data weakened the dollar.       \par     On the other hand, any substantial slowdown in major\par economies could keep interest rates at record lows for longer.\par The mixed messages kept markets struggling for clarity.\par      "The advance has stopped for a while, but there has been no\par five or 10 percent correction," said Alvin Tan, a strategist at\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Societe Generale\f28 \uc2 \f2 . "And that is a result of the environment of\par very low volatility we have at the moment."\par     Investors were also digesting an unexpected drop in euro\par zone business and consumer confidence data and a\par small rise in German inflation numbers. Both are important for\par the  \f28 \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's future policy.\par     The decline in sentiment confirmed recent PMI data,  and\par underscored the low gear the 18-country bloc's economy was stuck\par in and the need for European leaders to accelerate growth plans.\par    \par  \par     OIL \par     Not all market moves fitted with conventional wisdom.\par     Oil, usually the most sensitive to Middle East unrest, was\par on course for its biggest weekly drop since January, since the\par fighting in Iraq has not yet spread to the south where most of\par the country's oil is produced. \par     At $113 a barrel, prices have dropped nearly $3 from a\par nine-month high of $115.71 hit on June 19. "The exaggerated fear\par premium is being priced out," said \f28 \uc2 \f2 Carsten Fritsch\f28 \uc2 \f2 , a senior oil\par and commodities analyst at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Commerzbank\f28 \uc2 \f2  in Frankfurt. \par     Asian shares had followed Wall Street lower overnight.\par Japan's benchmark Nikkei fell 1.5 percent and regional\par markets, with the exception of Wellington and Mumbai, all posted\par losses.\par     The 10-year U.S. Treasuries yield held near a four-week low\par at 2.52 percent in European trading.\par     The U.S. dollar index also stuck tight to one-month\par lows reached on Wednesday. At 80.150, it stood just above its\par low of 80.091. The dollar also hovered around a five-week low of\par 101.315 yen. \par     The euro was little changed at $1.3618 after the euro\par zone data. \par     Standout performers were the New Zealand dollar, which was\par close to its highest in nearly three years on rate hike bets,\par and the Canadian dollar, which rose to a six-month high as\par investors sought out higher-yielding \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par     Among emerging markets, Central European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  were\par broadly weaker against the euro . But the\par Russian rouble rose to a five-month high as traders\par wagered that any new Western sanctions would be flimsy.\par     "In all honesty. I don't think that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Russia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par further in Ukraine," said Peter Elam Hakansson, fund manager\par Chairman of East Capital. \par     "If they did, the rest of Ukraine would immediately rush to\par join \f28 \uc2 \f2 NATO\f28 \uc2 \f2  and ally with the West... That would mean they would\par lose any influence in Ukraine, and that is not what they want."\par         \par \par  (Reporting by Marc Jones; Editing by Larry King)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-6-27T14:19:18.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020140627ea6r006o2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
