\par\par \b \uc2 Russia Economic Growth Impressive At First Glance -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,312 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Juni 2014\par 14:20\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par Markettalk.eu@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1220 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ]--Russia economic growth looks impressive at first glance but several factors help to explain the rise. Economic growth accelerated in May year-on-year compared with April, but the lack of growth month-on-month suggests that the growth spurt is due to an extra working day in May 2104 compared to the previous year. Also, May 2013 was a bad month for the economy, and so the year-on-year comparison is using a low base. Russia GDP was up 1.3% in May year-on-year, up from 1.1% in April. (Alexander.Kolyandr@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 8:18 EDT - Treasury prices add to last session's rally ahead of a GDP release, widely expected to show 1Q shrank even more than previously reported. With an outcome along the lines of -2%, many traders say not to expect that to drive further gains in Treasurys outside of an initial "sticker shock" reaction. Instead, an afternoon $35B sale of 5-year notes may put a cap on price increases. One risk to the auction: a flare-up of strife in Iraq that fuels a safe-haven bid. Ten-year notes up 4/32 to yield 2.572%. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; @cynthialin_dj)\par \par \uc2 1201 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] \uc2 Intesa Sanpaolo\uc2  says that the slight drop in Italy's June consumer confidence is normal considering the strong gains since March, which had brought the survey to its highest level in more than four years. Notes how the biggest drops in June are those that posted the biggest increases in recent months, such as general economic assessment and future outlook. The bank says the overall trend of consumer confidence heading north appears set, especially after Wednesday's retail sales in April rising, indicating a boost in household spending. (liam.moloney@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1155 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Some gold stored in Thailand has moved to Singapore as a result of political unrest, Malca-Amit's international commodities director Allan Finn says on the sidelines of an industry conference in Singapore. Finn didn't say how much but says some clients have moved gold from Thailand to "more stable" Singapore only to ship it back buyers in Thailand when there are orders. Gold storage in Singapore is "pretty tight at the moment" and Finn will welcome more space. Malca-Amit provides secure storage for diamonds and precious metals around the world including in Singapore and Bangkok. (huileng.tan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1153 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] WPP's (WPP.LN) trading update confirms recent comments from the CEO of a slight acceleration in performance during the second quarter compared to the first, however, there remains a relatively significant drag from \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2 , says Exane. "The small bad news may come from \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  which has had, for the first five months, an 8.6% impact on revenues, although we think this has been well flagged," says Exane. Exane adds that it is worth noting that performance in the U.S. and EM is accelerating over 1Q but Western Continental Europe is slowing down. Exane rates the stock at outperform. Shares fall 0.8%.(nina.bains@wsj.com; \uc2 Twitter\uc2 : @NinaBains1)\par \par \uc2 1151 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] In a survey of 87 investors with $420 billion assets, \uc2 Bank of America Merrill Lynch\uc2  fund managers turned noticeably more bearish on EM \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  from May to June. "There are big consensus favorites like MXN, although INR, while still popular, has lost a few followers over the last month," the survey found. The Fed remains the driver of EM markets and a UST selloff is still by far the bigger tail risk for EM assets. Investors see Argentina as having the highest default probability over the next 12 months, followed by Ukraine and Iraq.(chiara.albanese@wsj.com @chiaraalbanese)\par \par \uc2 1143 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ]--The ruble is turning into a short term buying opportunity and \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  advises staying short TRY/RUB. "The ruble is to be supported in the near term on reduction in sanction risks, high oil prices and tight monetary policy," strategists at the bank say, adding that, over the medium-term, the outlook for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains poor with Russian growth sub-1% and the non-oil current account balance in decline, while political events and sanctions this year may have a lasting impact on capital flows. (chiara.albanese@wsj.com @chiaraalbanese)\par \par \uc2 1140 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ]-- \uc2 Bank of America Merrill Lynch\uc2  economist Vadim Khramov says that geopolitical risk has subsided dramatically in Eastern Europe following Russian President Vladimir Putin's request to rescind authorization for military force in Ukraine. "From now on, we believe that the conflict will remain localized. Tensions are of course still high, but we certainly don't expect military \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he says, despite the fact that pro-\b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  rebels shot down a military helicopter with a heat-seeking missile, killing nine soldiers Tuesday. He adds that capital markets activity, in the form of \uc2 Sberbank\uc2  selling a EUR1 billion bond to international investors on Monday, signals the situation is normalizing and investors are not shying away from exposure to Russian assets. \uc2 Gazprombank\uc2  is thought to be preparing to issue bonds later this week too. (josie.cox@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1134 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Thailand's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Wednesday released audited financial statements for the year ended Dec. 31 confirming a net loss of 129.48 billion baht ($3.98 billion)--up 18% year over year. The \uc2 Bank of Thailand\uc2 's accumulated loss rise to 442.65 billion baht. "The losses are due to \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates and interest payments from liquidity-absorbing monetary policy \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," it says. After baht appreciation peaked last March the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakened against the dollar for the rest of the year. The baht depreciated 6.8% in 2013. (nopparat.chaichalearmmongkol@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 7:18 EDT - It says something when a 0.6% drop in S&P 500 is considered something to worry about. Some folks are arguing that this is the beginning of an inevitable correction. The VIX "fear gauge" options volatility picked up too but is still extremely low. Some believe that the low volatility is a sign of investor complacency. But there has been no shortage of external shocks with the potential to trigger some kind of selloff, with little follow through. History tells us that one day some bit of news is going to trigger a panic, but what? And until then, shorter-term history tells us that if this latest slippage stabilizes and calm is restored, investors will turn the autopilot of gradual stock accumulation back on. (michael.casey@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1108 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The \uc2 Bank of Spain\uc2  says its expects Spain's moderate GDP growth to continue in the second quarter, driven by an increase in consumer demand and investment in capital goods. In its June economic bulletin, the banking supervisor does not provide a 2Q GDP growth forecast. Still, notes that many indicators are pointing to a gradually healing economy. Jobs in the services sector are increasing; the index of industrial production for capital goods is rising; household confidence is improving. And people are buying more new cars than a year ago. It's a basis for mild optimism. (ilan.brat@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1053 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The short-covering rally being seen in EUR/GBP should run out of steam soon says \uc2 Barclays\uc2 , who is looking to scale into a short position above 0.8035, with stops just above 0.8080. Now trading at 0.8015, a break back below 0.80 will turn the bank immediately bearish. (gary.stride@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 June 25, 2014 08:20 ET (12:20 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140625ea6p001g1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
