\par\par \b \uc2 EM Investors Turning Bearish On  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 , BAML -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,534 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Juni 2014\par 13:51\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par Markettalk.eu@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1151 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] In a survey of 87 investors with $420 billion assets, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of America Merrill Lynch\f28 \uc2 \f2  fund managers turned noticeably more bearish on EM \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  from May to June. "There are big consensus favorites like MXN, although INR, while still popular, has lost a few followers over the last month," the survey found. The Fed remains the driver of EM markets and a UST selloff is still by far the bigger tail risk for EM assets. Investors see Argentina as having the highest default probability over the next 12 months, followed by Ukraine and Iraq.(chiara.albanese@wsj.com @chiaraalbanese)\f28 \par \par \uc2 1143 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ]--The ruble is turning into a short term buying opportunity and \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  advises staying short TRY/RUB. "The ruble is to be supported in the near term on reduction in sanction risks, high oil prices and tight monetary policy," strategists at the bank say, adding that, over the medium-term, the outlook for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains poor with Russian growth sub-1% and the non-oil current account balance in decline, while political events and sanctions this year may have a lasting impact on capital flows. (chiara.albanese@wsj.com @chiaraalbanese)\par \par \uc2 1140 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ]-- \uc2 Bank of America Merrill Lynch\uc2  economist Vadim Khramov says that geopolitical risk has subsided dramatically in Eastern Europe following Russian President Vladimir Putin's request to rescind authorization for military force in Ukraine. "From now on, we believe that the conflict will remain localized. Tensions are of course still high, but we certainly don't expect military \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he says, despite the fact that pro-\b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  rebels shot down a military helicopter with a heat-seeking missile, killing nine soldiers Tuesday. He adds that capital markets activity, in the form of \uc2 Sberbank\uc2  selling a EUR1 billion bond to international investors on Monday, signals the situation is normalizing and investors are not shying away from exposure to Russian assets. \uc2 Gazprombank\uc2  is thought to be preparing to issue bonds later this week too. (josie.cox@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1134 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Thailand's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Wednesday released audited financial statements for the year ended Dec. 31 confirming a net loss of 129.48 billion baht ($3.98 billion)--up 18% year over year. The \uc2 Bank of Thailand\uc2 's accumulated loss rise to 442.65 billion baht. "The losses are due to \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates and interest payments from liquidity-absorbing monetary policy \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," it says. After baht appreciation peaked last March the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakened against the dollar for the rest of the year. The baht depreciated 6.8% in 2013. (nopparat.chaichalearmmongkol@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 7:18 EDT - It says something when a 0.6% drop in S&P 500 is considered something to worry about. Some folks are arguing that this is the beginning of an inevitable correction. The VIX "fear gauge" options volatility picked up too but is still extremely low. Some believe that the low volatility is a sign of investor complacency. But there has been no shortage of external shocks with the potential to trigger some kind of selloff, with little follow through. History tells us that one day some bit of news is going to trigger a panic, but what? And until then, shorter-term history tells us that if this latest slippage stabilizes and calm is restored, investors will turn the autopilot of gradual stock accumulation back on. (michael.casey@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1108 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The \uc2 Bank of Spain\uc2  says its expects Spain's moderate GDP growth to continue in the second quarter, driven by an increase in consumer demand and investment in capital goods. In its June economic bulletin, the banking supervisor does not provide a 2Q GDP growth forecast. Still, notes that many indicators are pointing to a gradually healing economy. Jobs in the services sector are increasing; the index of industrial production for capital goods is rising; household confidence is improving. And people are buying more new cars than a year ago. It's a basis for mild optimism. (ilan.brat@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1053 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The short-covering rally being seen in EUR/GBP should run out of steam soon says \uc2 Barclays\uc2 , who is looking to scale into a short position above 0.8035, with stops just above 0.8080. Now trading at 0.8015, a break back below 0.80 will turn the bank immediately bearish. (gary.stride@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1047 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Hungary will continue cutting its main interest rate in July, for the 24th month in a row, say 15 of the 16 economists in a Wall Street Journal poll. The anniversary rate cut of the two-year easing cycle will be another 10bp clip to the policy rate, taking it to 2.20%, all but two of the 15 analysts expecting a cut say. The dissenters forecast a 5bp easing, which would be a novelty in Hungary's monetary policy history. Most economists see the bottom of the current easing cycle to come between 2.00% and 2.20% in the coming months, with \uc2 Bank of America Merrill Lynch\uc2  the only one forecasting a rate bottom below the 2.00% mark with its 1.80% call. All economists expect an extended period of flat rates to follow before a tightening cycle starts sometime next year. Hungary cut its main rate Tuesday by 10bp to 2.30%, as expected. (margit.feher@wsj.com @margitfeher)\par \par \uc2 1046 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Malaysia shares end lower with the benchmark KLCI slipping 0.1% to 1889.55. New listing \uc2 Icon Offshore\uc2  (5255.KU) closes up 3.2% to MYR1.91 compared with a MYR1.85 IPO price. Any retreat in the KLCI--which hit new record of 1892.33 Tuesday--is likely cushioned by the expectations of first-half window dressing, stronger economic data from the U.S. and China, Hong Leong IB says. Volume is 1.73 billion shares. Felda Global (5222.KU) ends down 1.6% at MYR4.34, \uc2 YTL Corp\uc2  (4677.KU) down 1.3% at MYR1.58 and SapuraKencana (5218.KU) down 1.1% at MYR4.36. (jason.ng@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0000 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Supports at $0.9350 and $0.9322 have moved into range on the AUD/USD charts, as Tuesday's weakness left Monday's $0.9447 high stranded. Doubts have been raised as the validity of the two-month bull pennant continuation pattern on the daily chart, and a break below $0.9322 would morph the continuation pattern into a rectangle, creating downside risk to the $0.9202/08 lows. The $0.9447 high would only become the focus on a break above $0.9417. AUD/USD is at $0.9366.\par \par \uc2 -By Francis Bray, \uc2 Dow Jones Chief Technical\uc2  Analyst For Europe.\par \par \uc2 (francis.bray@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 (This article is general financial information, not personalized investment advice, as it does not consider the unique circumstances affecting an individual reader's decision to buy or sell a specific security or \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \uc2 Dow Jones\uc2  does not warrant the accuracy, completeness or timeliness of the information in this article, and any errors will not be made the basis for any claim against \uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 . The author does not invest in the instruments or markets cited in this article.)\par \par \uc2 1005 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The September Bund future is ploughing steadily higher into new contract highs above 146.33 on Wednesday, in search of an upside objective at 146.54. This push higher extends the broader advance from the June 5 reaction low at 144.03, and the sub-dividing bull waves have produced higher objectives at 146.90 and 147.08. The all-time traded high at 147.20 on the continuation chart would then become the focus. Support at 146.08 means limited scope for corrective weakness. The September Bund future is at 146.45. -By Francis Bray, \uc2 Dow Jones Chief Technical\uc2  Analyst For Europe.(francis.bray@dowjones.com) (This article is general financial information, not personalized investment advice, as it does not consider the unique circumstances affecting an individual reader's decision to buy or sell a specific security or \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \uc2 Dow Jones\uc2  does not warrant the accuracy, completeness or timeliness of the information in this article, and any errors will not be made the basis for any claim against \uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 . The author does not invest in the instruments or markets cited in this article.)\par \par \uc2 1002 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  reckons a case is building for an early rate increase from the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2 . The bank has analysed recent speeches and interviews by members of the Monetary Policy Committee and thinks five members are leaning towards an early rise. There is still a difference in terms of support for tighter monetary policy though: Ian McCafferty and Martin Weale have a strong view for an early increase while Mark Carney, \uc2 Andy Haldane\uc2 , and Kristin Forbes have a more mild view. (neelabh.chaturvedi@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires