\par\par \b \uc2 GBP/USD Longs Look Vulnerable Into FOMC, Says Citi -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,435 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Juni 2014\par 11:32\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par Markettalk.eu@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0932 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ]--While the minutes from the Bank of England MPC's June meeting were overall supportive for sterling, the lack of hawkish surprises could see GBP/USD longs take some profit off the table ahead of tonight's FOMC event, says Citi's Head of European G10 strategy Valentin Marinov. Apart from confirming Governor Mark Carney's Mansion House message that risks of an earlier rate hike are more balanced, and also noting some upside risks to 2H GDP, Mr. Marinov says the June minutes were not that much different from those seen in May. GBP/USD now trades at 1.6945 from a post-minutes high of 1.7004. (gary.stride@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0908 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ]--The "taper tantrum" that erupted last year when the Federal Reserve first hinted at monetary tightening offers the best clues to how stocks will perform as quantitative easing ends and rates rise, but investors should beware relying on it as a template, says Angus Parker, \uc2 HSBC\uc2 's head of developed equities. "There's a different valuation starting point for some of the sectors," he says, and sectors that have performed better, such as health care, "actually could be more exposed than they were last time." On the other hand, the Fed's shot across the bow means that some sectors which were sold off heavily, such as emerging market-focused consumer staples, will probably perform better this time around, he adds. The Fed is expected to cut monthly asset purchases by $10bn to $35bn at its meeting today. (gregor.hunter@wsj.com; \uc2 Twitter\uc2 : @gregorhunter)\par \par \uc2 0902 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] GBP/USD takes a small hit after slightly less hawkish-than-expected BoE minutes from the June MPC meeting, trading down at a fresh day low of 1.6935. \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  technical analyst Karen Jones expects any further weakness to find support at 1.6905 and terminate in the 1.6875 area, to be followed by a rebound toward 1.70. Although not favored, Jones says a break below 1.6840 would alleviate immediate upside pressure, below 1.6672 would completely negate it. GBP/USD now at 1.6952. (gary.stride@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0901 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The Bank Of Russia gave the Ruble more freedom this week by curtailing \b \highlight22\uc2 forex interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The move is widely expected to boost the Ruble's volatility but it has a few silver linings. \uc2 Unicredit\uc2  says that a reduction in \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be positive for \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's sovereign ratings as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will now spend less from its \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, which lost more than $40B in the 1H of 2014 and slid to $467.2B by early June. The higher flexibility or weakness of the Ruble should also prop up the current account surplus by trimming imports which get more expensive as the Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakens. But the Ukrainian crisis may postpone the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's plan to let the Ruble float freely starting from 2015. \uc2 Unicredit\uc2  says geopolitical tensions are likely to persist throughout this year, limiting cap inflows, while the weaker Ruble may fuel cap flight at a time when the economy needs investment. (Andrey.Ostroukh@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0854 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Hong Kong equities fall for a third straight day as declining house prices unnerve real-estate stocks and China's state audit body unearths financial irregularities in two state-owned banks and the country's sovereign wealth fund. A gauge of new house prices shows month-over-month declines during May in 35 of 70 cities polled by the \uc2 Chinese National Bureau of Statistics\uc2 . "The cooling house price inflation will increase worries of a bigger slump in the Chinese market, but it is the rising corporate defaults that are posing the bigger risk," says Nordea. The Hang Seng Index ends 0.1% lower at 23181.72 while the Hang Seng China Enterprises Index slips 0.2% to 10444.41. Bank of China (3988.HK) falls 0.5% while \uc2 Agricultural Bank of China\uc2  (1288.HK) closes flat. (gregor.hunter@wsj.com; \uc2 Twitter\uc2 : @gregorhunter)\par \par \uc2 0844 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] China government bonds are mixed as IPO subscriptions tighten liquidity, but a successful bond auction lifts investors' appetite for long-term bonds. The five-year government bond yield is two basis points higher at 3.90%, the ten-year government bond yield is two basis points lower at 4.07%. Seven companies are opening share sales to investors this week, absorbing liquidity from markets. The benchmark seven-day repo rate is up at 3.09% from 3.08% late Tuesday. The finance ministry auctioned a 10-year bond at 4.00%, lower than the secondary market yield and slightly lower than market expectations for 4.01%. (wynne.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0844 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Sterling now trades lower than it did before BoE minutes, after printing a new high for the day on their release. GBP/USD now at 1.6952 from 1.6965 beforehand and 1.7004 immediately afterwards. EUR/GBP climbs to 0.7996 from a low of 0.7969 and 0.7989 where it went into the event. (gary.stride@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0842 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ]--Earlier this month the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  launched a series of measures to tray and ensure low inflation in the euro zone doesn't become entrenched. While it's too early to truly assess the impact, Christian Schulz, economist with \uc2 Berenberg Bank\uc2  in London, says the policies are already showing some desired short term effects. The euro exchange rate is lower, the rates euro zone banks charge to lend to each other have tumbled, and banks have so far not sped up their excess liquidity reduction, despite a negative deposit rate. christopher.lawton@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 0838 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ]--The exact timing of the BOE's first rate hike will depend on how much spare capacity the MPC's members believe still exists in the economy. The more slack has been reduced, the more continued growth will generate inflationary pressures that have to be countered by a rate move. But the MPC sees "considerable uncertainty" over its estimate of spare capacity of 1% to 1.5% of GDP, and the minutes also said there were "a range of views" on the subject. All agreed that the Bank rate shouldn't be raised until there is "more evidence" of slack being absorbed, but the minutes suggest that point will be reached by different members at different times. paul.hannon@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 0837 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The USD/TWD is largely steady ahead of the U.S. Federal Reserve's monetary policy statement later in the day. The pair closes at 30.071 compared with 30.066 late Tuesday. The local \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is believed to have bought the USD around 30.005, two traders say. One of the traders adds that the pair will likely remain capped under 30.050 Thursday, as exporters are keen on selling the greenback at these prices while inflows to local stocks will also take steam off the pair. She expects the USD/TWD to find strong support at 29.95-29.98 zone. The Taiex ends up 0.4% at a six-year high. (aries.poon@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0837 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ]--The BOE isn't sure how long-lived an apparent slowdown in the UK housing market will be, seeing the recent decline in mortgage approvals as a reflection of new, tighter mortgage rules whose impact could be sustained. But the fall in approvals could also reflect lack of supply, itself consistent with rising prices. The MPC concludes by noting that, for now, it's up to the FPC to decide on the necessary action. paul.hannon@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 0835 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ]--According to the minutes of their June meeting, members of the BOE's Monetary Policy Committee were "surprised" by the low probability investors attached to a 2014 rate move. That was before Governor Mark Carney delivered his Mansion House speech, in which he drew attention to the possibility of a hike this year. Clearly, he was speaking on behalf of the MPC. But possible is not likely, and MPC members were also agreed on the "considerable costs" in terms of lost output of a premature hike, as well as the difficulty of reversing such a move. paul.hannon@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 June 18, 2014 05:32 ET (09:32 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140618ea6i0015d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
