\par\par \b \uc2 Ruble Flexibility Good For Sovereign Ratings -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 200 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Juni 2014\par 11:01\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 0901 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The Bank Of Russia gave the Ruble more freedom this week by curtailing \b \highlight22\uc2 forex interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The move is widely expected to boost the Ruble's volatility but it has a few silver linings. \uc2 Unicredit\uc2  says that a reduction in \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be positive for \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's sovereign ratings as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will now spend less from its \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, which lost more than $40B in the 1H of 2014 and slid to $467.2B by early June. The higher flexibility or weakness of the Ruble should also prop up the current account surplus by trimming imports which get more expensive as the Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakens. But the Ukrainian crisis may postpone the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's plan to let the Ruble float freely starting from 2015. \uc2 Unicredit\uc2  says geopolitical tensions are likely to persist throughout this year, limiting cap inflows, while the weaker Ruble may fuel cap flight at a time when the economy needs investment. (Andrey.Ostroukh@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par Markettalk.eu@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 June 18, 2014 05:01 ET (09:01 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140618ea6i0011q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
