\par\par \b \uc2 Russia to Let Ruble Float from 2015\b0\par\par\uc2 By Andrey Ostroukh \par \uc2 335 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Juni 2014\par 10:06\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 MOSCOW--The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  said Tuesday it is cutting \b \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  aimed at smoothing ruble volatility, partially removing a defense implemented in early March before Moscow annexed Crimea.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  plans to let the ruble float freely from 2015 and will focus on taming inflation through interest rates. The regulator would still \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if needed to maintain financial stability.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which keeps the Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  within a seven-ruble floating band against a euro-dollar basket, said it had trimmed its daily \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by $100 million, its second such step in the past month.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sells foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  when the ruble weakens toward the upper boundary of the trading corridor and buys dollars and euros when the ruble approaches the lower end. It carries out no \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the middle of the trading band.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said Tuesday it is also scaling down its so-called accumulated \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , or the amount needed for a 5 kopeck shift in the ruble trading band. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will now shift the band after selling $1 billion from reserves if the ruble moves to the upper boundary of a trading corridor against a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  basket made up of dollars and euros.\par \par \uc2 On March 3, when there was a massive selloff in the Russian market fueled by fear of Western sanctions and military \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Crimea, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised the amount of accumulated \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to $1.5 billion from $350 million in an attempt to contain the ruble's drop to all-time lows.\par \par \uc2 The ruble weakened following the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's latest decision, losing 0.4% on the day and hovering at 40.36 against the euro-dollar basket. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said the decision to trim \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  shouldn't have any impact on the Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Write to Andrey Ostroukh at andrey.ostroukh@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 June 17, 2014 04:06 ET (08:06 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140617ea6h000s1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
