\par\par \b \uc2 Russia Business Forecast Report Q3 2014\b0\par\par\uc2 1,282 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Juni 2014\par 14:59\par \uc2 PR Newswire (U.S.)\par \uc2 PRN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9  2014  PR Newswire Association LLC.  All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 LONDON, June 9, 2014 /PRNewswire/ -- Reportbuyer.com has added a new market research report:\par \par \uc2 Russia Business Forecast Report Q3 2014\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.reportbuyer.com/energy_utilities/oil_gas/russia_business_forecast_report_q3_2013_4.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.reportbuyer.com/energy_utilities/oil_gas/russia_business_forecast_report_q3_2013_4.html}}}\par \par \uc2 Core Views\par \par \uc2 The outlook for domestic demand in Russia is grim in 2014, as tensions between Russia and the West over Ukraine in Q114 weigh on businesses' and consumers' confidence. Following a modest rebound in 2015 in growth, driven by subsiding geopolitical tensions, we reiterate our expectation for a moderate deceleration in private consumption growth and subdued pace of fixed investment growth over the next few years. Although inflation spiked to 7.3% y-o-y in April, up from 6.1% y-o-y in January, we reiterate our view that inflation will subside by yearend. Subsiding inflationary pressures will allow the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Russia\uc2  to cut the main policy rate from 7.50% to 7.25% by end-2014.\par \par \uc2 While the rouble might continue to pare its post-March 3 losses in the short term, we reiterate our long-term bearish outlook for the rouble. We forecast the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to trade within a long-term depreciatory trend, and to average RUB35.20/USD in 2014 and RUB35.60/USD in 2015. The main drivers underpinning our view are the deteriorating outlook for Russia's current account surplus, subdued demand for Russian real and financial assets and the planned transition of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Russia\uc2  (CBR)'s exchange rate policy towards free float by 2015.\par \par \uc2 Major Forecast Changes\par \par \uc2 We have revised down our forecast for real GDP -- to 1.0% in 2014, from 1.9% previously, and to 1.5% for 2015 from 2.1% previously on account of the worsening outlook for domestic demand in 2014 due to the Ukraine crisis. We expect fixed investment in Russia to contract in 2014 -- by 1.5%, whereas previously we expected modest growth of 0.5%, as geopolitical tensions between Russia and the West over Ukraine in Q114 shattered Russia's image in the investors' eye.\par \par \uc2 We expect Russia's current account surplus to moderately expand in 2014 on the back of the worsening outlook for imports on the back of the weaker outlook for domestic demand -- to 1.8% for 2014, up from 1.6% in 2013, whereas previously we expected a modest expansion. Nevertheless, from 2015 onwards, the current account\par \par \uc2 will resume its narrowing trajectory, and reverse into deficit by 2017, driven by the deteriorating outlook for hydrocarbon exports, which are poised for structural deceleration both in volumes and prices.\par \par \uc2 Executive Summary . 5\par \par \uc2 Core Views 5\par \par \uc2 Major Forecast Changes ..5\par \par \uc2 Key Risks To Outlook 5\par \par \uc2 Chapter 1: Political Outlook 7\par \par \uc2 SWOT Analysis .. 7\par \par \uc2 BMI Political Risk Ratings .. 7\par \par \uc2 Domestic Politics . 8\par \par \uc2 US Trade Sanctions Unlikely Without Invasion .8\par \par \uc2 The US will refrain from trade sanctions on Russia, unless the latter invades Eastern Ukraine. We think an invasion is still unlikely, as\par \par \uc2 the military, political, and economic costs to Russia would be too high.\par \par \uc2 Table: Politic al Over view .8\par \par \uc2 Foreign Policy 9\par \par \uc2 Russia And The West: Multi-Year Cool Period Ahead ..9\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Ukraine will usher in a much cooler period in relations with the West for several years. A major improvement in\par \par \uc2 relations is unlikely until President Putin leaves office, and even then, military and security-related obstacles will prevail over diplomatic\par \par \uc2 opportunities.\par \par \uc2 Long-Term Political Outlook .. 13\par \par \uc2 Political Competition To Increase Over The Next Decade ..13\par \par \uc2 Russia's main political challenges over the coming decade will be implementing institutional and economic reform, managing the\par \par \uc2 country's demographic decline, containing the Islamist insurgency in the North Caucasus, and limiting the fall-out from a deterioration in\par \par \uc2 relations with the West.\par \par \uc2 Chapter 2: Economic Outlook . 17\par \par \uc2 SWOT Analysis 17\par \par \uc2 BMI Economic Risk Ratings 17\par \par \uc2 Economic Activity . 18\par \par \uc2 Ukraine Crisis Darkening Russia's Growth Outlook 18\par \par \uc2 The outlook for domestic demand in Russia is grim in 2014, as tensions between Russia and the West over Ukraine in Q114 weigh on\par \par \uc2 businesses' and consumers' confidence.\par \par \uc2 table: Economic Activity 18\par \par \uc2 Fiscal Policy .. 20\par \par \uc2 Questionable Long-Term Fiscal Sustainability ..20\par \par \uc2 Russia's low public debt and deficit levels mean no threats to the government's financing needs over the next two years. However, the\par \par \uc2 state's sizeable footprint in the economy will continue to underpin elevated spending, which along with accumulated contingent liabilities\par \par \uc2 and running down savings funds raise questions over Russia's long-term fiscal sustainability.\par \par \uc2 table: Fiscal policy ..20\par \par \uc2 Monetary Policy .. 21\par \par \uc2 Monetary Easing By End-2014 ..21\par \par \uc2 Following a spike in inflation in Q114 driven by sharp rouble depreciation, we forecast inflation to abate in H214. Subsiding inflationary\par \par \uc2 pressures will allow the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Russia\uc2  to cut the main policy rate from 7.50% to 7.25% by end-2014.\par \par \uc2 table: Monetary Policy 22\par \par \uc2 Balance Of Payments .. 23\par \par \uc2 Current Account Expansion In 2014 To Be Short-Lived 23\par \par \uc2 While we expect Russia's current account surplus to moderately expand in 2014 on the back of the worsening outlook for imports, we\par \par \uc2 maintain that it is poised to turn into deficit by 2017. Russia's ability to avoid a balance of payments crisis will depend crucially on its\par \par \uc2 ability to ramp up net financial inflows, which remains uncertain given its history of scant levels of foreign direct investment and high\par \par \uc2 private capital outflows.\par \par \uc2 table: Current Account ..23\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Forecast . 25\par \par \uc2 RUB: Tactically Bullish, Strategically Bearish 25\par \par \uc2 TABLE: BMI \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCY\b0 \highlight\uc2  FORECAST ..25\par \par \uc2 Chapter 3: 10-Year Forecast . 27\par \par \uc2 The Russian Economy To 2023 27\par \par \uc2 Major Long-Term Growth Challenges ..27\par \par \uc2 We forecast Russian growth to slow markedly over our 10-year forecast horizon, with average real GDP growth of just 2.7% between\par \par \uc2 2016 and 2023. During this period the economy will shift increasingly towards domestic demand, with private consumption to account\par \par \uc2 for 57.1% of total GDP in 2023, up from 49% in 2012. While we expect Russia to converge towards developed standards of wealth,\par \par \uc2 structural risks including a weak business environment, poor institutional capacity and declining population pose major challenges to\par \par \uc2 long-term growth.\par \par \uc2 tABLE: Long-Term Macroeconomic Forecasts .27\par \par \uc2 Chapter 4: Business Environment 29\par \par \uc2 SWOT Analysis 29\par \par \uc2 BMI Business Environment Risk Ratings .. 29\par \par \uc2 Business Environment Outlook 30\par \par \uc2 Institutions . 30\par \par \uc2 TABLE: BMI BUSINESS AND \b \highlight22\uc2 OPERATION\b0 \highlight\uc2  RISK RATINGS 30\par \par \uc2 TABLE: BMI LEGAL FRAMEWORK RATING .31\par \par \uc2 TABLE: LA BOUR FORCE QUAL ITY ..32\par \par \uc2 TABLE: EMERGING EUROPE - ANNUAL FDI INFLO WS ..33\par \par \uc2 Infrastructure 34\par \par \uc2 TABLE: TRADE AND INVESTMENT RATINGS 34\par \par \uc2 Market Orientation . 35\par \par \uc2 TABLE: Top Export Destinations .36\par \par \uc2 Market Orientation . 37\par \par \uc2 Chapter 5: Key Sectors 39\par \par \uc2 Defence 39\par \par \uc2 TABLE: Defence Expenditure 39\par \par \uc2 Freight Transport 42\par \par \uc2 TABLE: Rail Freight .45\par \par \uc2 TABLE: Road Freight ..45\par \par \uc2 TABLE: Inland Waterw ay Freight .45\par \par \uc2 TABLE: Air Freight 46\par \par \uc2 TABLE: Maritime Freight .46\par \par \uc2 Other Key Sectors . 47\par \par \uc2 table: Oil and Gas Sector Key Indicators .47\par \par \uc2 table: Telecoms Sector Key Indicators .47\par \par \uc2 table: Infrastructure Sector Key Indicators 47\par \par \uc2 table: Food and Drink Sector Key Indicators ..48\par \par \uc2 table: Pharm a Sect or Key Indic ators ..48\par \par \uc2 table: Autos Sector Key Indicators ..48\par \par \uc2 Chapter 6: BMI Global Assumptions .. 49\par \par \uc2 Global Outlook . 49\par \par \uc2 Growth Increasingly Polarised ..49\par \par \uc2 Table: Global Assumptions ..49\par \par \uc2 Table: Developed States , Real GDP Growt H, % 50\par \par \uc2 Table: BMI VERSUS BLOO MBERG CONSENSUS REAL GDP GROWTH FORECASTS, % .. 50\par \par \uc2 Table: Emerging Markets , Real GDP Growth , % ..51\par \par \uc2 Read the full report:\par \par \uc2 Russia Business Forecast Report Q3 2014\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.reportbuyer.com/energy_utilities/oil_gas/russia_business_forecast_report_q3_2013_4.html"