\par\par \b \uc2 Bank Of Russia Deputy Head Yudaeva: Inflation Issue Still "Very Serious"\b0\par\par\uc2 By Andrey Ostroukh \par \uc2 428 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Juni 2014\par 16:07\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 MOSCOW--The harsh measures undertaken by the Bank of Russia to ensure financial stability three months ago have had a positive impact on the country's economy, but inflation still remains "a very serious issue," the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's first deputy chairwoman said Monday.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  unexpectedly raised interest rates and boosted \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the ruble on the first trading day of March, when the ruble hit record lows on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets after Russia's takeover of the Crimean peninsula of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Ksenia Yudaeva said that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's steps were aimed at calming "unjustified and irrational panic," among households who rushed to ditch rubles after seeing that banks increased their conversion rate to 53 rubles per euro and 39 rubles per dollar over the weekend of March 2-3. On the last trading day of February, the ruble had traded at 35.9 against the dollar and at 49.6 against the euro.\par \par \uc2 Speaking at a financial markets working group in the lower house of parliament, Ms. Yudaeva shrugged off claims that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's monetary tightening had a negative impact on the economy. She said that Russia's economy is suffering from a lack of structural reforms, while investment activity is weak regardless of the interest rates levels.\par \par \uc2 Despite concerns that Russia's economy may contract this year as a result of capital flight, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised rates again in late April, providing additional support to the battered ruble, which has now recovered to levels seen before the turmoil stirred by Moscow's annexation of Crimea.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Yudaeva said the measures undertaken by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent financial destabilization have had a positive effect on the economy. As an example, she referred to corporate lending growth, which accelerated to 17%-18% from a year earlier in April-May, compared with less than 13% growth seen in late 2013.\par \par \uc2 Stubbornly high inflation remains a serious problem, Ms. Yudaeva said. Taming inflation is a structural reform that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is working on now, which ultimately would prop up trust in the ruble and ensure macroeconomic stability.\par \par \uc2 She said that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is now revising its consumer inflation forecast, which had initially envisaged annual inflation slowing to the target below 6.5% by the end of the year from 7.3% seen in April.\par \par \uc2 Write to andrey.ostroukh@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 June 02, 2014 10:07 ET (14:07 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140602ea620021j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
