\par\par \b \uc2 NZD/USD Could Head Toward 0.8430 -\uc2 Westpac\uc2  -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,475 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Mai 2014\par 23:32\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 2132 GMT - With NZD/USD breaking "emphatically" through key support of 0.8525, the pair could move toward 0.8430 during the coming week, \uc2 Westpac\uc2  says in its morning note. Pair at 0.8498 vs 0.8539 late Wednesday in New Zealand. "The US dollar index rose to a fresh two-month high. That was mainly because EUR fell to a fresh three-month low at 1.3589," firm notes. \uc2 Westpac\uc2  says mid term, the RBNZ tightening cycle will remain supportive, "but much is already price in," and furthermore, "the market is expecting an RBNZ track downgrade on June 12, which should lend a negative bias to NZD/USD such that a fall to below 0.8400 is possible." (lucy.craymer@wsj.com; @lucy_craymer)\par \par \uc2 15:43 EDT - For now, damage averted. Brazil's Supreme Court decides to indefinitely postpone a judgment that may result in hundreds of billions of reais in losses for the country's banks, according to \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  estimates. The case is about whether saving-accounts yields were underpaid decades ago, when hyperinflation wreaked havoc on Brazil's economy. Government-controlled banks are likely to be hit harder, in case the justices side with the plaintiffs, mainly consumer groups. The banks deny any wrongdoing. (paulo.trevisani@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 14:45 EDT - \uc2 Walt Disney\uc2  (DIS) is selling three-year bonds to yield just 0.20 percentage point more than comparable Treasurys, one of the lowest spreads for three-year debt over the last two and a half years, according to S&P Capital IQ LCD, which began tracking high-grade bonds in late 2011. The tightest spread came in March of this year, when \uc2 Exxon Mobil\uc2  (XOM) sold three-year bonds with a 0.15 percentage point spread. \uc2 Disney\uc2  is selling $2B in debt overall on Wednesday, including five-year and 30-year bonds. (mike.cherney@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 14:37 EDT - The bid keeps coming for long-end Treasurys, with 10-year notes now up 23/32 to yield 2.436% and the 30-year bond up 1 12/32 to yield 3.294%. Jake Lowery, portfolio manager at Voya Investment Management, says today's strength highlights how buyers are looking at "relative value rather than a need for yield." Pointing to the still-wide gap between 10-year Treasury and German bund yields--last a historically high 1.1 percentage point--Lowery says "global fixed income looks relatively expensive." He warns that within this global context, swings in other developed-nation bond markets will have an impact on Treasurys. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; @cynthialin_dj)\par \par \uc2 13:48 EDT - This afternoon's $13B sale of two-year US floating-rate notes posted nearly identical bidding stats as the auction in April. The 4.69 cover just tops April's 4.64, while both booked a 6.9 bps spread. The key difference in today's sale is where the supply landed. Investors outside the dealer community took 51% of the total offering, up from 39% in April and 37% in March, likely reflecting more active participation by investment funds at home and abroad. Dealers have said that the much of the floaters they bought in prior auctions were eventually distributed to clients, but now it looks like investors are getting comfortable enough with the market to buy in the primary market. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; @cynthialin_dj)\par \par \uc2 13:14 EDT - Demand at the US's 5-year note auction wasn't too bad considering the government offered a 1.513% yield, the lowest rate on the maturity since November. Bids submitted were enough to cover the $35B sale 2.73 times, a bit below the recent average of 2.84. Direct buyers took a subpar 10% of the sale, while indirect bidders took an above-average 50%. The mixed reception shows hesitance about buying into yields at such low levels, but how Treasurys remain a relatively decent buy compared to other countries' low-yielding bonds. Prices hold at session highs after the auction, with 10-year notes up 21/32 to yield 2.443%. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; @cynthialin_dj)\par \par \uc2 1647 GMT [Dow Jones]--Despite the huge challenges Ukraine has to maintain its territorial integrity, \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2  thinks the situation will stabilise and the disbursement of the IMF/EU package should provide substantial support to Ukrainian assets. "We upgrade our view on Ukraine to overweight," says SocGen's Regis Chatellier. On the other hand, he expects that a direct military \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  in eastern Ukraine is unlikely, and in that context, heavy sanctions against Moscow have become a low probability scenario. "Although we still could see selling pressures in the very short term, Russian bonds are poised to outperform in the coming weeks, as we think that most of the geopolitical risk affecting Russia is now behind us, and largely reflected into market valuations. We turn tactically overweight Russia," he says. (chiara.albanese@wsj.com @chiaraalbanese) \u61548\'F0\'6C\par \par \uc2 1613 GMT [Dow Jones]--Japanese investors have turned into net buyers of foreign bonds over the past month, according to data released by Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry Of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "Investors started the year out as net sellers, shedding foreign bonds in 12 out of the first 16 weeks of 2014 for a total net outflow during that period of $57.6 billion," notes BBH. Over the last 4 weeks inflows have been $26.4 billion of net foreign bond purchases, with nearly $14 billion over last week only. "Despite the increased purchases of foreign bonds by Japan investors, Uridashi issuance has not really picked up," notes analyst BBH Win Thin. Marketed to retail investors, Uridashi bonds represent a small slice of the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. "But we believe that the observed trends in this segment can reflect those of the larger Japan investment community as well," he says. (chiara.albanese@wsj.com @chiaraalbanese). \u61548\'F0\'6C\par \par \uc2 1601 GMT [Dow Jones]--Trading volumes in G-10 \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  are rising ahead of next week's ECB meeting, mainly as increased activity among central banks and private clients, says \uc2 Credit Agricole\uc2 . "The EUR was sold last week, still on the back of firm expectations of the ECB considering more aggressive monetary policy action at next week's meeting," the bank said in its client monitor. Hedge funds and private clients selling the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  suggests rising upside risk should the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  disappoint elevated market expectations, the bank adds. (chiara.albanese@wsj.com @chiaraalbanese). \u61548\'F0\'6C\par \par \uc2 1530 GMT [Dow Jones]--In the short term, investors might be attracted back into Russian markets as growth looks resilient, supported by the tight labor market, a high oil price and a weak ruble, says \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2 . But while the short-term outlook is positive, medium-term the picture is different. "We see a lasting impact on investor willingness to hold Russian assets, and a lack of domestic reform appetite, which would keep future Russian growth prospects subdued, capped at around 2% per annum," says \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2 . (chiara.albanese@wsj.com @chiaraalbanese).\par \par \uc2 11:22 EDT - \uc2 Barclays\uc2  says several themes appear to be simmering in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets which could disrupt the "current benign market environment." Uncertainty over ECB and BOE policy is unlikely to force an immediate break from the recent market tranquility, but do "suggest that a period of more compelling price action may soon be upon us," \uc2 Barclays\uc2  says. Firm says there's more uncertainty over the timing of BOE action after a hawkish shift seen in the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's May meeting minutes, which sets up a bigger potential shakeup once action is taken. Investors are also expecting the ECB to cut rates June, though it's uncertain if it'll be paired with a more-aggressive stimulus program. (don.curren@wsj.com; @dbcurren)\par \par \uc2 1521 GMT [Dow Jones]--Tensions between the Ukraine and Russia are relaxing, but in the long term, Russia will likely keep breathing down its neighbor's neck, says Jan Dehn, head of research at Ashmore. "We expect the de-escalation that began following the second round of Western sanctions against Russia to continue, albeit unevenly," he writes in a note to clients. He explains that it will be uneven "because Russia is likely to continue to exercise its influence in Eastern Ukraine in order to extract concessions from the Poroshenko administration to ensure that Ukraine does not become a Poland-like, staunchly pro-Western country." Longer-term, Mr. Dehn says, Russia is still hoping to create the conditions that push Ukraine towards joining the Eurasian Economic Union --an EU-like institution with the ruble as the main \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for setting investment and trade transactions. (josie.cox@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 May 28, 2014 17:32 ET (21:32 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140528ea5s003zs\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
