\par\par \b \uc2 Russia Slashed Its U.S. Treasury Holdings in March -- WSJ Blog\b0\par\par\uc2 By Cynthia Lin   \par \uc2 492 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Mai 2014\par 19:09\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Russia reduced the amount of U.S. Treasurys held at home in March as the government faced a tumbling \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and threats of sanctions.\par \par \uc2 The Treasury's latest international capital flow report shows Russia's Treasury securities holdings tumbling to $100 billion in March, from $126 billion in February. That's a record monthly decline for Russia and the largest drop by far for any one nation that month.\par \par \uc2 The move came as Russia advanced its claim on Ukraine's Crimea territory, a move condemned by the U.S., resulting in a number of sanctions against certain institutions and wealthy individuals.\par \par \uc2 Sanctions not only threatened the \uc2 Russian government\uc2 's ability to manage it offshore assets, but also battered its stock and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets in March. With the ruble depreciating to an all-time low of RUB37 per dollar, the Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  hiked rates and sold some $22 billion in U.S. dollars that month to prop up its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "This supports the fact that when they were \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the [\b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 ] market, \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  must have sold some Treasurys," said Shyam Rajan, interest rate strategist at \uc2 Bank of America\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Speculation first arose in March that Russia was rearranging its Treasury assets ahead of sanctions when the Federal Reserve reported a record weekly drop in so-called custody holdings--U.S. Treasurys held at the \uc2 New York Fed\uc2  for foreign accounts.\par \par \uc2 Those holdings plunged by $105 billion in the week ended March 12. It wasn't clear at that point who was behind the move because the Fed's custody data aren't broken down by nation. But the drop sparked chatter that it was likely Russia taking the Treasurys it owns off U.S. soil to avoid having assets potentially frozen, either selling the securities or shifting them to custodian accounts elsewhere.\par \par \uc2 The Fed custody holdings have since recovered from that record drop, though Thursday's TIC data support the theory that Russia was active in managing it Treasury holdings in March.\par \par \uc2 To be sure, the drop in Russia's holdings doesn't necessarily mean all securities were sold. TIC data is calculated based off where the securities are held, so Russia could have simply parked its Treasurys with an offshore custodian. Treasury yields were fairly steady in March, with the 10-year yield ending the month up 0.1 percentage point to 2.72%.\par \par \uc2 "We believe this was due to the shift in custodians prior to heightened tensions in Ukraine, demonstrated by large inflows into Luxembourg and Switzerland," says Nomura Securities.\par \par \uc2 Luxembourg's holdings increased by $8.3 billion in March, and Switzerland's rose $7.5 billion. Belgium, another popular custodial destination, saw its holdings rise $40 billion. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  More at The Wall Street Journal's MoneyBeat blog, \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://blogs.wsj.com/moneybeat/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://blogs.wsj.com/moneybeat/}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 May 15, 2014 13:09 ET (17:09 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140515ea5f003y8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
