\par\par \b \uc2 Column: Ukraine Needs A Lifeline -- Now -- Marco Rubio\b0\par\par\uc2 799 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Mai 2014\par 01:02\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par A13\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    (FROM THE WALL STREET JOURNAL 5/6/14) \par    By Marco Rubio \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Editor's Note: Mr. Rubio, a Republican from Florida, is a member of the Senate Foreign Relations Committee.)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Ukraine's future as a sovereign nation is under threat as Russia continues to behave aggressively in the region. President Obama and European leaders are considering imposing broader economic sanctions to punish Vladimir Putin for unlawfully annexing Crimea. They should do it quickly.\par \par \uc2 This would make it clear to the Kremlin, which is deeply involved in managing Russia's economy and military, that belligerent behavior has consequences. But in addition to hurting Russia with sanctions, the U.S. should strengthen Ukraine economically. This will help the country withstand Mr. Putin's attempted takeover.\par \par \uc2 One way to increase the chances of a prosperous future in Ukraine is to strengthen the nation's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the hryvnia -- for a stable monetary foundation is necessary for economic growth. Even as the value of the Russian ruble has fallen almost 9% against the dollar in 2014, Mr. Putin takes comfort in knowing that the hryvnia is doing worse: It has plummeted 35% against the dollar since January. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's weakness is part of his plan to bring Ukraine to its knees.\par \par \uc2 This is where America and our European allies can throw a wrench into Mr. Putin's designs, rather than standing idly by as the hryvnia collapses under physical and psychological intimidation from Russia. We should encourage the establishment of a Ukrainian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board, an institutional arrangement that anchors the value of national money to a more stable \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Under a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board, the hryvnia would be convertible into the dollar or the euro at a fixed rate, and backed by Ukraine's own hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves. The \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  would supplement the reserves with a special-purpose loan arrangement.\par \par \uc2 A \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board would help Ukraine's money become as reliable and stable as the world's dominant reserve \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The effects would ripple throughout the economy: Foreign investors could have confidence that the hryvnia is not in a death spiral, and Ukrainians would know that Mr. Putin cannot annihilate the value of their personal savings. Such stability would encourage the nation under siege to maintain its faith in free people and free markets.\par \par \uc2 Equally important: Moscow would immediately face the dismaying reality that Ukraine's money is suddenly far more dependable than its own. Russia is already on a spending blowout to save the ruble as economic conditions deteriorate: \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has spent more than $23 billion \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets since January. On April 25, the bank raised its key interest rate by 50 basis points to 7.5%, a desperate attempt to tamp down the inflationary effects of a weakening ruble. Monetary policy is not Russia's forte in global affairs, and so the U.S. and Europe should use their advantage strategically to hurt a vulnerable adversary.\par \par \uc2 In an April 10 letter to Treasury Secretary Jack Lew, Sen. Ted Cruz (R., Texas) and I suggested that the Treasury Department work with the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  to create a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board to help Ukraine transition to more solid economic footing. The IMF has a long track record of supporting \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  boards and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board-like systems. Johns Hopkins economist Steve Hanke, for instance, has designed such systems for Estonia, Lithuania, Bosnia-Herzegovina and Bulgaria. Estonia has enjoyed a remarkable political and economic transformation thanks in large part to a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board introduced in 1992, which helped the country transition to the euro zone.\par \par \uc2 Strengthening the national \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and bringing a disciplined monetary regime would send a powerful signal of support to Kiev as the new government attempts to bring economic reform and democratic institutions to Ukraine. Most important, establishing a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board would avoid wasting IMF and other international assistance, which includes U.S. taxpayer dollars, on an unstable economy.\par \par \uc2 At a time when it is vital to bolster trust in democracy and free markets, Ukrainian leaders and the IMF should recognize the value of a stable monetary base and financial system. The IMF should provide the technical experience necessary to ensure that the system, which would be run by Ukrainian monetary authorities, is implemented properly. The IMF, after all, was established for the precise purpose of managing a fixed-rate exchange system anchored by a gold-linked dollar.\par \par \uc2 America must continue to urge innovative measures to demonstrate that we stand with the Ukrainian people, who have already shown courage and conviction in confronting Mr. Putin's menacing threats. And so we should extend to them the safe-haven benefits of having a trustworthy sovereign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 May 05, 2014 19:02 ET (23:02 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140505ea55003vc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
