\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-Russian assets rise on sanctions relief\b0\par\par\uc2 726 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 April 2014\par 18:43\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2014 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Sanctions over Ukraine targeted individuals not sectors\par \par \uc2 * Market had priced in more severe sanctions - analyst\par \par \uc2 * Share index gains knocked back after further unrest (Adds details and quotes, updates prices)\par \par \uc2 By Jason Bush and Zlata Garasyuta\par \par \uc2 MOSCOW, April 29 (Reuters) - Russian asset prices rose on Tuesday, reflecting investors' relief that new Western sanctions against Russia over Ukraine were less severe than many had feared, but shares gave up some gains after further civil unrest in eastern Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 At the close the rouble-denominated MICEX share index was up 0.4 percent to 1,305 points, while the dollar-denominated RTS had risen 1.2 percent to 1,153 points.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, when the new sanctions were announced, the MICEX rose 1.6 percent and the RTS 2.2 percent, having fallen 5.6 percent and 6.7 percent respectively the previous week.\par \par \uc2 "The new sanctions have caused a sigh of relief," Golden Hills Capital analyst Natalia Samoilova said in a morning note.\par \par \uc2 "The market had priced in far more serious sanctions against whole sectors of the economy."\par \par \uc2 The United States spared major listed companies or whole sectors, instead targeting individuals close to President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  and companies belonging to them, none of which are traded on the stock exchange.\par \par \uc2 Russian indexes had been up 1.2-1.8 percent shortly after opening, but later shed part of the gains, knocked by the seizure of another government building by pro-Russian protesters in the eastern Ukrainian city of Luhansk.\par \par \uc2 "Today the market reacted to the events in Luhansk. The fall in prices was small but all the same it was there," said Yury Khilov, head of trading \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  at \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  in Russia.\par \par \uc2 "As with previous days this month: in the absence of news, on thin volumes, the market rises, and with the appearance of alarming reports the volumes immediately increase."\par \par \uc2 SHORT MONEY\par \par \uc2 Analysts said the question remained how the dispute will be resolved.\par \par \uc2 "The fall in the market last week was extremely serious: investors were preparing for the worst-case development of events, therefore they are now attempting to return to purchases," Investcafe analyst Mikhail Kuzmin said in a note.\par \par \uc2 "If the Ukrainian authorities return to active military \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  and there are new victims, this could again destabilise the conflict situation."\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  has said that it has the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  militarily in Ukraine to protect Russian speakers, while the West has warned of much tougher sanctions if Russia invades.\par \par \uc2 "Of course we are only seeing short money. People are buying for a couple of weeks, no longer," said Alexei Evsyutin, responsible for retail sales at BCS brokerage.\par \par \uc2 Shares in leading oil company Rosneft, which had fallen 1.4 percent on Monday after its CEO \uc2 Igor Sechin\uc2  was sanctioned by the United States, rebounded by 1.7 percent on Tuesday. It was helped by a board recommendation late on Monday to boost its dividend pay-out by 60 percent.\par \par \uc2 The bond markets shrugged off a downgrade of six major state-owned companies by \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  announced late on Monday, following its downgrade of the sovereign on Friday, instead reacting positively to the improved sentiment over sanctions.\par \par \uc2 The yield on \uc2 Gazprom\uc2  2019 bonds fell to 5.74 percent, down from 6.01 on Monday. The yield on \uc2 Russian Railways\uc2 ' 2017 bonds fell to 4.57 percent from 5.09 percent the previous day.\par \par \uc2 The rouble was also stronger on Tuesday, hitting a 10-day peak. At 1600 GMT it was 0.6 percent stronger at 35.62 against the dollar and by 0.8 percent to 49.21 against the euro, strengthening by 0.7 percent to 42.74 against the dollar-euro basket.\par \par \uc2 The rouble was also helped by German inflation figures, which were lower than forecast, making monetary stimulus by the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  more likely and weakening the euro.\par \par \uc2 Traders said that given the decline in Russian country risk, market players were reluctant to extend their foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  long positions over the Russian May holidays, leading to sales of \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the days leading up to May 1.\par \par \uc2 "Paying 4 kopecks to extend the longs is reckoned to be too much," said a dealer at a large Western bank. (Additional reporting by Vladimir Abramov; Editing by Alison Williams)\par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-5-2T16:43:29.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020140429ea4t00avq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
