\par\par \b \uc2 European Morning Briefing: Stock Markets Seen Cautiously Higher\b0\par\par\uc2 4,521 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 April 2014\par 06:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SNAPSHOT:\par \par \uc2 ========\par \par \uc2 European bourses seen slightly higher, bunds and gilts mixed; euro, spot gold, oil lower\par \par \uc2 -Obama, Putin Talk as Unrest Roils Ukraine\par \par \uc2 -Britain, Germany, France Urge De-escalation In Ukraine, UK Says\par \par \uc2 -EU to Expand Targeted Sanctions on Russian Officials\par \par \uc2 -ECB's Noyer: Euro Appreciation Over Last Year 'Not Appropriate'\par \par \uc2 -UK Retail Sales Fall in March As Easter Holiday Distorts Results\par \par \uc2 -Telefonica Offers to Create Rival to Ease E-Plus Deal -FT\par \par \uc2 WATCH FOR: U.K. inflation, house prices; German ZEW indicator; EU foreign trade; U.S. CPI, NY manufacturing, housing index, oil data, Fed's Rosengren\par \par \uc2 EUROPEAN OUTLOOK & U.S./ASIAN SUMMARIES:\par \par \uc2 ========\par \par \uc2 Investors will see cautiously higher European stock markets, with bunds and gilts likely mixed, as they train their eyes on the Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 The leaders of Britain, Germany, and France agree that the occupation of government buildings in eastern Ukraine is illegal and have called on all parties, particularly Russia, to de-escalate the situation, the \uc2 British government\uc2  said Monday. And the U.S. stepped up efforts to dissuade \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Ukraine's increasingly unstable east, with President Barack Obama telling President Vladimir Putin Monday that a diplomatic solution to the crisis is still possible even as he warned against further escalation.\par \par \uc2 Japanese and Australian stocks moved higher on Tuesday, recovering from heavy selling in recent sessions, while Hong Kong shares were lower ahead of Wednesday's headline growth figures from China.\par \par \uc2 The region took a positive lead from the U.S., where Wall Street snapped a two-day losing streak on Monday, as investors welcomed a robust rise in March retail sales. That suggested that consumer demand in the world's largest economy is reheating after a cold winter.\par \par \uc2 "Energy in the market has dwindled, as traders are having a hard time finding decent catalysts," said Hiroichi Nishi, general manager of equities at SMBC Nikko Securities. "Sell pressure has lessened compared to last week, but renewed buying pressure may take some time to gather momentum."\par \par \uc2 Wall Street futures and Treasurys were little changed, with the euro, spot gold and oil all lower.\par \par \uc2 STOCKS:\par \par \uc2 ======\par \par \uc2 Ukraine events will keep markets on edge. Although Wall Street ended higher, the market saw selling toward the close. As a result, European markets may start cautiously and only slightly higher.\par \par \uc2 Ahead of the opening, IG has called the FTSE up 7 at 6590, the DAX up 16 at 9355 and the CAC up 2 at 4386.\par \par \uc2 "In short, further conflict in the country is a certainty; gold, the EUR, the DAX, the ruble and the Micex will move on flare-ups and are the markets to watch as this story hits the front pages once more," Evan Lucas, strategist at IG said.\par \par \uc2 Wall Street futures were little changed Tuesday, after stocks rebounded snapping a two-day losing streak, as investors focused on strong retail sales in March and positive earnings from banking giant \uc2 Citigroup\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "The economy is continuing to plod along, one foot in front of the other," said Joe Heider, a regional managing principal at Rehmann Financial, which manages about $2 billion. "People are reacting [to economic news] on a day-to-day business."\par \par \uc2 Analysts said earnings and, perhaps more important, sales results continue to hold the key to near-term direction, particularly in the tech sector, which has been hardest hit as investors grow worried that valuations have been stretched too far.\par \par \uc2 "There's no telling how long the current bout of volatility will last, or how deep the pullback will go. But if history is any guide, markets may overshoot on the way down," said Jerry Webman, chief economist at Oppenheimer Funds, in a note.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 :\par \par \uc2 ======\par \par \uc2 The euro continued to decline against the dollar in Asia after European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Mario Draghi ratcheted up his warning Monday about the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strength, saying a further rise in the exchange rate would prompt additional monetary easing from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "Talk is cheap, and we've seen a great deal of talk from European policy makers, but little action," said Omer Esiner, chief market analyst at \uc2 Commonwealth \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "Despite these comments growing increasingly pointed, they are becoming less effective at bringing down the euro."\par \par \uc2 Mr. Draghi's comments may have capped further gains in the euro, but the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is unlikely to head much lower unless the ECB announces new stimulus measures, Mr. Esiner said.\par \par \uc2 Adding to the recent chorus, ECB Governing Council member Christian Noyer said the recent appreciation of the euro isn't appropriate.\par \par \uc2 From the technical view, the euro's decline has encouraged bears, said BofA technical strategist MacNeil Curry. EUR/USD's reversal from 1.3904 and slide to 1.3820 Monday "warns that the tide is turning in favor of the U.S. dollar," he says. But patience is required, he said, as a "formidable" base of euro buyers is seen around 1.3708. A break below that is required for euro bears to take control, which opens the door to the pair's September low of 1.3104, then 1.2777.\par \par \uc2 BONDS:\par \par \uc2 =====\par \par \uc2 Bunds may start mixed with the important ZEW business sentiment survey for April due. The business expectations component is likely to have slipped to 45 from 46.6 in March, while the current situation measure rose to 51.8 from 51.3.\par \par \uc2 German business sentiment took a knock in March amid the Crimean crisis. And though the current state of the economy is healthy--some recent estimates suggest the German economy was growing at nearly a 3% annualized pace in the first quarter--the outlook is cloudy, not least because of developments beyond Germany's eastern borders.\par \par \uc2 Gilts may start mixed Tuesday, with inflation numbers on tap. Consumer and producer price inflation will remain well behaved in the U.K., which will leave the Bank of England content to stick to its lower for longer policy, even as the economy booms.\par \par \uc2 Treasury bond prices were little changed Tuesday, after markets fell for the first time in three sessions on Monday as a strong retail sales report reduced investors' appetites for ultra-safe \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  debt.\par \par \uc2 Despite the rise, the yield traded near a six-week low and has fallen from 3.03% at the end of last year, the highest level since July 2011.\par \par \uc2 "I think the current quarter is when we have the greatest risk of returning to 3%" regarding the 10-year Treasury yield, said William O'Donnell, head of \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  bond strategy at RBS Securities Inc. "A weather-led bounce-back in growth numbers like today's retail sales figures could shift expectations back to stronger growth in the coming few months."\par \par \uc2 Later in the day, focus will turn to U.S. March CPI, seen rising 0.1% on the month. If the consensus forecast is correct, disinflationary pressures will be shown to be alive, perhaps prompting the Fed to stay on a very gradual path of unwinding its stimulus program.\par \par \uc2 "We still have situation in Ukraine supporting the bond market," said Larry Milstein, head of government and agency trading at R.W. Pressprich & Co. in New York. "So far Ukraine have not acted, but things could escalate quickly if they do act, particularly with thousands of Russian troops on the border."\par \par \uc2 In Japan Tuesday, government bonds were little changed. Japan's economy minister said the market had built its own expectations over additional easing measures by the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ENERGY:\par \par \uc2 =======\par \par \uc2 Oil futures were lower in Asia as stability in Ukraine's geopolitical landscape remains fragile. WTI prices have been overstretched in the last few days, with few fundamental factors to underpin prices, analysts said. China's GDP data, due Wednesday, is expected to "move back to the forefront of trader attention," oil advisory firm Ritterbusch said in a report. The market will also be closely following developments in Libya. Without a stable government in place to negotiate, it remains unclear when a new deal could be reached between the rebels and the government to restart oil exports. Ritterbusch expects WTI and Brent to move towards $100/bbl and $105/bbl, respectively, though developments in Ukraine remain "extremely difficult to predict."\par \par \uc2 May Nymex crude was at $103.51/bbl, down 23 cents, while Brent crude was off 19 cents at $108.88/bbl Monday.\par \par \uc2 METALS:\par \par \uc2 ======\par \par \uc2 Base metals could remain in sideways mode in Asia as traders are likely to wait for China's GDP data Wednesday before placing bets. Weak economic data from China have weighed on base metal prices in recent months.\par \par \uc2 LME 3-month copper was down $3 at $6,667.\par \par \uc2 However, nickel could remain underpinned on worries about supply woes following a ban imposed by Indonesia in January on the export of unprocessed ore. Also, tensions between Russia and the West are helping to support prices as Russia is one of the biggest suppliers of the metal, which is used for making stainless steel.\par \par \uc2 LME 3-month nickel was up $87 at $17,881.\par \par \uc2 Spot gold was displaying a soft tone in Asia in the wake of a stronger dollar on good U.S. retail sales data. The intraday technical picture for gold was negative. The precious metal may test support at $1,318.45, and then at $1,313.74.\par \par \uc2 Spot gold was recently at $1,322.08/oz, down $5.91.\par \par \uc2 Write to Dennis Baker at dennis.baker@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 15 Apr 2014 00:00 ET  European Morning Briefing: DJ Dominant Headlines; Other Top Stories\par \par \uc2 DJ Dominant Headlines:\par \par \uc2 Stockbrokers Who Fail Test Have Checkered Records\par \par \uc2 Onex, Cineplex Team Recently Offered More Than $1 Billion For \uc2 Dave & Buster's\uc2  -- Source\par \par \uc2 China Is Losing Its Taste for Gold\par \par \uc2 Citigroup CFO Says Alleged Fraud Involves Up To $30 Million\par \par \uc2 Other Top Stories:\par \par \uc2 Australia's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 : Australian Dollar High By Historic Standards\par \par \uc2 A recovery in the Australian dollar to its highest levels since the end of last year, leaving it again "high by historical standards," means it is now less of a spur to recovery in the economy, the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said in the minutes to its latest meeting.\par \par \uc2 Rio Tinto\uc2  Iron-Ore Output Drops in Adverse Weather\par \par \uc2 Rio Tinto PLC\uc2  (RIO) said iron-ore output fell in the first quarter of its fiscal year due to bad weather that swept across Australia's Pilbara mining hub.\par \par \uc2 Obama, Putin Talk As Unrest Roils Ukraine\par \par \uc2 The U.S. stepped up efforts to dissuade \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Ukraine's increasingly unstable east, with President Barack Obama telling President Vladimir Putin Monday that a diplomatic solution to the crisis is still possible even as he warned against further escalation.\par \par \uc2 UK Retail Sales Fall in March As Easter Holiday Distorts Results\par \par \uc2 U.K. retailers reported a decline in sales in March compared with the same month last year, a survey showed Tuesday, as the timing of the Easter break skewed the comparison.\par \par \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  Logs Higher Profit, Beats Estimates\par \par \uc2 Citigroup Inc\uc2 . Chief Executive Michael Corbat vowed to find an "industrial-strength" solution to the regulatory problems dogging the bank.\par \par \uc2 15 Apr 2014 00:00 ET  European Morning Briefing: Market Prices, Data Calendar\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par DJIA     16173.24 +146.49 +0.91% \par Nasdaq    4022.69  +22.96 +0.57% \par S&P 500   1830.61  +14.92 +0.82% \par FTSE 100  6583.76  +22.06 +0.34% \par DAX       9339.17  +23.88 +0.26% \par CAC40     4384.56  +18.70 +0.43% \par Closing \par  \par Dow Future   16094.00 -2.00  0.0% \par NASDAQ Future 3470.00 -0.25  0.0% \par S&P Future    1824.40 -0.10  0.0% \par As of 0330 GMT \par  \par Nikkei       14024.71 +114.55 +0.82% \par Hang Seng    22920.89 -117.91 -0.51% \par S&P/ASX 200   5406.70  +47.80 +0.89% \par MumbaiSensex 22628.96  -86.37 -0.38% \par Kospi         1997.99   +0.97 +0.05% \par ShanghaiComp  2114.36  -17.18 -0.81% \par Taipei        8905.65  +48.23 +0.54% \par As of 0325 GMT \par  \par USD/JPY 101.84-86  0.00% \par Range   101.96 101.82 \par EUR/USD 1.3815-18 -0.03% \par Range   1.3821 1.3814 \par GBP/USD 1.6714-18 -0.07% \par Range   1.6730 1.6716 \par USD/CHF 0.8802-06 +0.07% \par Range   0.8805 0.8798 \par AUD/USD 0.9394-98 -0.29% \par Range   0.9424 0.9390 \par EUR/JPY 140.72-74 -0.02% \par Range   140.87 140.70 \par EUR/GBP 0.8265-66 +0.04% \par Range   0.8266 0.8259 \par EUR/CHF 1.2162-66 +0.04% \par Ra