\par\par \b \uc2 Surge in Russian capital outflows adds to economic woes\b0\par\par\uc2 700 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 April 2014\par 14:08\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2014 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Net capital outflow reaches $63.7 billion in first quarter\par \par \uc2 * Reflects demand for dollars from companies and households\par \par \uc2 * Coincides with slumping investment and falling growth\par \par \uc2 By Jason Bush\par \par \uc2 MOSCOW, April 9 (Reuters) - A surge in capital outflows from Russia underscores how international tensions over Ukraine are adding to the woes of Russia's already struggling economy.\par \par \uc2 Russia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has published balance of payments data that showed an estimated $63.7 billion in net capital outflow in the first three months of this year - as much as the $63 billion in outflows seen during the whole of 2013.\par \par \uc2 The bank first published a figure of $50.6 billion on Tuesday, but later included an adjusted total to add $13.2 billion in \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  transactions between banks and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which analysts say provides a clearer picture of total capital flight.\par \par \uc2 The surge coincides with slumping investment and a sharp deterioration in business confidence, as forecasters slash economic growth forecasts after \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's annexation of Crimea and warnings it could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  further in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 "Of course it's the conflict with Ukraine and the uncertainty about potential (military) \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in eastern Ukraine that triggered such big outflows," said Alexei Devyatov, economist at Uralsib.\par \par \uc2 Alfa Bank\uc2  economist Natalia Orlova said the Ukraine crisis was only one factor, as outflows were already strong even before the threat of Russian military \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Domestic investment by Russian companies fell by 7 percent in January and by 3.5 percent in February, compared with a year earlier, illustrating how rising capital outflows coincide with declining willingness to invest in production in Russia.\par \par \uc2 "Overall the net capital outflows reflect general concern about financial stability, and also declining incentives to invest in large \b \highlight22\uc2 operational\b0 \highlight\uc2  activities by Russian companies," she said.\par \par \uc2 Recent surveys suggest that business confidence is now the lowest since 2008.\par \par \uc2 Reacting to such trends, President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  has called for the business climate to be improved "substantially" and "at an accelerated pace", though previous such exhortations have delivered scant results.\par \par \uc2 FLIGHT TO DOLLARS\par \par \uc2 VTB Capital economist Vladimir Kolychev said the outflow was being driven by companies and ordinary households, which have been rushing to convert their rouble savings into dollars.\par \par \uc2 "The most important factor behind this huge capital outflow was savings dollarisation," he said.\par \par \uc2 Households' demand for foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the first quarter, $19.6 billion, was the highest since the fourth quarter of 2008, while banks' foreign assets reached $35 billion, also close to levels last seen in the 2008 financial crisis.\par \par \uc2 "Of course we see substantial increases in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchases by Russian residents as a reaction to rouble weakness - which was triggered by capital outflow. These things are self-supporting in a sense," said Uralsib's Devyatov.\par \par \uc2 The surge in capital outflows was among the factors leading the Economy Ministry to cut its official economic growth for this year, joining a wave of organisations that have slashed Russian growth forecasts because of the Ukraine crisis.\par \par \uc2 Deputy Economy Minister Andrei Klepach said on Tuesday the ministry forecast growth at either 0.5 percent or 1.1. percent in 2014 - with the higher figure conditional on fiscal stimulus steps.\par \par \uc2 Both forecasts represent a sharp reduction compared with the previous official 2014 growth forecast of 2.5 percent, and are also below last year's disappointing growth rate of 1.3 percent.\par \par \uc2 The Economy Ministry estimated that for the year capital outflows would reach $100 billion, implying that the bulk of this year's outflows have already occurred.\par \par \uc2 However, some other forecasters are gloomier. The \uc2 World Bank\uc2  warns that the total for the year could reach $150 billion.\par \par \uc2 "Looking forward you may take two views," said VTB Capital's Kolychev. "The first one is that the peak of dollarisation is over, which means that the rouble should stabilise in the next year."\par \par \uc2 "The opposite view is that we are only in the middle of this geopolitical mess (over Ukraine). That means more pressure on the rouble as locals continue to run away from local assets." (additional reporting by Oksana Kobzeva; editing by Elizabeth Piper and Giles Elgood)\par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-4-12T12:08:04.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020140409ea4900ju4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
