\par\par \b \uc2 Global Central Banking in 2014, A First Quarter Update For 23 Economies -- WSJ Blog\b0\par\par\uc2 7,030 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 April 2014\par 22:18\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Join WSJ's Hilsenrath and Blackstone for a live video chat about the ECB and other global central banks Thursday at 2 p.m. EDT {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://bit.ly/Pdpysf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://bit.ly/Pdpysf}}}\par \par \uc2 The world's central banks have navigated different currents in the first quarter of 2014 and are preparing themselves for a variety of challenges in the months ahead. Developed economies such as the U.S., Europe and Japan are still struggling with slow growth and uncomfortably low inflation. The Bank of England is holding interest rates low even as the U.K. recovery picks up steam. Emerging markets are bracing themselves for the Federal Reserve's gradual winding down of its extraordinary stimulus measures. \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has engineered a depreciation of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the yuan, amid signs of rising economic stress. Everybody is watching to see if the conflict between Russia and the West over Ukraine spills over to hurt the global economy.\par \par \uc2 Here's a guide to the individual outlooks for central banks around the world, compiled by our global staff of reporters and editors. Follow the links for more. And if you're still hungry, download our free e-book on new Fed Chairwoman Janet Yellen at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.wsj.com/fed"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.wsj.com/fed}}}\uc2 . \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -- U.S. Federal Reserve \par  \par  \par    -- \f28 \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par  \par  \par    -- \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan\f28 \uc2 \f2  \par  \par  \par    -- Bank of England \par  \par  \par    -- \f28 \uc2 \f2 People's Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2  \par  \par  \par    -- \f28 \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of Australia\f28 \uc2 \f2  \par  \par  \par    -- \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Brazil\f28 \uc2 \f2  \par  \par  \par    -- \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Canada\f28 \uc2 \f2  \par  \par  \par    -- \f28 \uc2 \f2 Czech National Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  \par  \par  \par    -- Danish National Bank \par  \par  \par    -- \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hungarian National Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  \par  \par  \par    -- \f28 \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of India\f28 \uc2 \f2  \par  \par  \par    -- Bank of Indonesia \par  \par  \par    -- Bank of Israel \par  \par  \par    -- \f28 \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of New Zealand\f28 \uc2 \f2  \par  \par  \par    -- \f28 \uc2 \f2 Norges Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  \par  \par  \par    -- \f28 \uc2 \f2 National Bank of Poland\f28 \uc2 \f2  \par  \par  \par    -- Bank of Russia \par  \par  \par    -- \f28 \uc2 \f2 South African Reserve Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  \par  \par  \par    -- Bank of Korea \par  \par  \par    -- Sweden's Riksbank \par  \par  \par    -- \f28 \uc2 \f2 Swiss National Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  \par  \par  \par    -- \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of The Republic of Turkey \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. Federal Reserve\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 By Victoria McGrane\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Janet Yellen took the helm of the Federal Reserve in February and appears to be steering it on a steady course for now, despite uncertainty about the exact timing of policy moves looming in the distance.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 She and her colleagues indicated at their March policy meeting, her first as chairwoman, they are likely to leave short-term interest rates near zero until next year. They also continued to scale back their monthly bond purchases in $10 billion increments at recent meetings and indicated they are likely to keep going and end them completely by later this year if the economy improves as expected.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 One change under Ms. Yellen was the Fed's new guidance about the likely path of short-term interest rates, which put less weight on the unemployment rate as a marker for when rate increases will start. Before the March meeting, the Fed had said it would start considering interest rate increases from near zero when unemployment fell to 6.5%, as long as the outlook for inflation remained below 2.5%. At the meeting, Fed officials dropped these numerical thresholds and said instead they would look at a broad range of economic information in deciding when to raise rates.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Just when that process might start will be the subject of great debate.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Ms. Yellen said in her inaugural press conference March 19 that the bond-buying program likely would end in the fall. That could mean it would end at meetings scheduled for either Oct. 28-29 or Dec. 16-17. The distinction matters to investors because the end of the bond-buying program is linked to the Fed's interest rate plans.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Fed said interest rates will remain near zero "for a considerable time" after the bond-buying program concludes. Ms. Yellen stirred up concern in markets when she suggested that the first rate increases might come about six months after the end of bond buying. While her projection came with many caveats, some investors took it as a sign that the Fed could start raising rates as early as April 2015, somewhat sooner than expected.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Several Fed officials have since stressed that six months is not a firm timeframe for their interest rate decision, which will depend on how the economy performs. And Ms Yellen emphasized in a speech in late March her continued strong support for the Fed's easy money policies, saying, "I believe it is appropriate for the Federal Reserve to continue to provide substantial help to the labor market."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Fed officials remain generally upbeat in their economic forecasts, despite signs of weakness in the winter that appeared at least partly due to harsh weather in some parts of the country. But they will be watching carefully for any signs of underlying softness that could hold back a rebound in coming months. One issue they are watching closely is inflation, which has remained well below their 2% target for 22 months.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 By Brian Blackstone\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Talk is cheap.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 That was the main lesson for \f28 \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  officials from the first quarter. They did a lot of talking about being ready to act with rate cuts or other stimulus measures, having a suite of instruments at their disposal and even expressed concerns about the high value of the euro, a rarity for central bankers who typically shy away from commenting on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But they took no new steps and investors fretted about their resolve to keep inflation at the ECB's 2% target. The euro pushed higher toward $1.40 in recent weeks, a level that threatens to make already super-low inflation rates even lower.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Annual inflation weakened further in March to 0.5%, the lowest rate since late 2009. This may force the ECB to weigh action as soon as its April 3 policy meeting. Most analysts think they will stand pat again, however, given that an unusually warm winter--which reduced energy and food prices--and the late timing of the Easter holiday were a temporary drag on prices\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Of the ECB's remaining tools--interest rate cuts, bank funding measures and large-scale asset purchases--rate cuts would likely be the first option. This may include reductions in the bank's 0.25% main lending rate and, more significantly, the deposit rate, currently zero.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Installing a negative deposit rate would put the ECB in largely unchartered waters for a major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . By effectively charging financial institutions to park overnight funds, the ECB might spur them to lend more to the private sector. The euro would probably weaken considerably if the ECB crosses that Rubicon.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Central bankers in Finland and Germany, who are considered among the most conservative of the ECB's 24-member council, have signaled they are open to this option. Going this route could then open the door to purchases of government bonds or, more likely, private-sector assets later this year if deflation risks intensify further.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The April 3 meeting will set the tone. If officials act on rates or take other measures, investors would have to reassess their view of the ECB as an overly cautious \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  loathe to joining the Federal Reserve and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan\f28 \uc2 \f2  in experimenting with innovative stimulus measures.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 If they hold fire again, their easing bias on interest rates and repeated pledges to act if needed could ring hollow.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 By George Nishiyama\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A year after rolling out monetary easing of a "different dimension" to bring an end to persistent deflation, things are looking good for the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan\f28 \uc2 \f2  as moderate inflation appears to have taken hold.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Data released in late March showed that the core consumer price index rose 1.3% in February from a year earlier, the third straight month of gains and a faster pace than most private economists had predicted.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But the BOJ's mission to achieve 2% inflation in two years will face its biggest challenge in April, when the national sales tax goes up to 8% from 5%, a move certain to drag down consumption and put downward pressure on growth and prices.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BOJ Gov. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Haruhiko Kuroda\f28 \uc2 \f2  has contended that any economic slowdown will be temporary. But the fiscal crunch comes in the absence of a strong pickup in exports, which BOJ officials had hoped would offset weaker consumption.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Mr. Kuroda believes exports will increase once emerging economies recover, but many private economists think that with structural changes such as Japanese manufacturers producing more overseas, any rebound will be limited.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 And the yen, whose fall of about 9% in the past year contributed to inflation by pushing up import prices, has recently stabilized, meaning less upward pressure on prices.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 So while the BOJ is sticking to a bullish outlook for prices -- saying the CPI excluding the effects of the tax increase will head toward 2% after the end of the year-- many private economists aren't so sure. They see the inflation rate falling after April and staying below 1% for the rest of the year, forcing the BOJ to take fresh action.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A survey of 40 economists by the private Japan Center for Economic Research showed that 36 thought the BOJ would have to ease again, with 14 expecting action around July.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bank of England\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 By Jason Douglas\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of England is expected to stay on cruise control through 2014 but don't be surprised to see deepening divisions emerge among officials over how far they can push the U.K. economy before inflation starts to take off.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The BOE's Monetary Policy Committee has kept its benchmark interest rate at a low of 0.5% since March 2009. With growth finally accelerating after a long stagnation, traders expect officials to begin the slow process of dismantling the extraordinary stimulus that's been in place for the past five years by raising rates early next year.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A critical judgment officials must make is how much slack there is in the U.K. economy. Spare capacity--for example, idle workers or factories-- usually means an economy has space to expand quickly without generating inflation. Wait too long and the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  might have to take drastic action to curb price gains; move too soon and they could kill the recovery.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1 Apr 2014 16:18 ET \par Global Central Banking in 2014, A First Quarter -2-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BOE officials said in February they reckoned the size of the shortfall was equivalent to between 1% and 1.5% of annual output. By keeping rates pinned near zero, officials hope to spur a quicker recovery and begin closing that gap.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But since they published that estimate it has become clear that there is a bigger range of opinion on the size of the gap than first appeared. Governor Mark Carney told lawmakers he thinks there might be more slack in the economy than that estimate implies; rate-setter Martin Weale has said he thinks there's probably less.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BOE officials are still setting policy under their August guidance, by which they pledged not to consider a rate rise at least until unemployment reached 7%. That threshold is likely to be crossed within months, after which these differences of opinion may spill over into outright disagreement on when to let borrowing costs rise. The BOE is also absorbing an influx of new blood on the committee: three new officials will be joining the panel within months.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Also on Mr. Carney's agenda in the months ahead: how to tame a potential housing boom. The Canadian-born governor has said the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will reach for its range of "macroprudential" tools to cool the market if a real-estate bubble forms.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 People's Bank of China\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 By Bob Davis\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  got its reform mojo working in the first quarter in a very Chinese way. It engineered a depreciation of the yuan through heavy government \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Then it doubled the trading band of the yuan to 2% from the day's starting point and let market forces go to work. With the Chinese economy headed downward, so did the yuan. \f28 \uc2 \f2 People's Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2  as market champion.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Now what? If the PBOC is really committed to letting the market have its way, the yuan might drop further. That's bound to alarm members of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Congress\f28 \uc2 \f2 , among others. In a U.S. election year, long-threatened sanctions on Chinese "\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  manipulation" could pass both chambers. The PBOC realizes that; most analysts figure if the yuan doesn't start strengthening on its own in the second quarter, the PBOC will \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to buck up the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  somewhat.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Having introduced uncertainty into \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  trading, the PBOC seems poised to encourage it elsewhere in the financial system. That means lobbying the government to look the other way when some bonds default--perhaps even one or two issued by a state-owned firm-- and letting investors take a hit.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 It could also mean starting to begin to put in place a bank deposit insurance scheme. In a system where everyone figures the government backstops everything, insuring only certain deposits up to a certain level -- probably 500,000 yuan (about $80,000) -- should put investors and institutions on alert that they better weigh risks far more carefully than they have in the past.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of Australia\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 By James Glynn and Rachel Pannett\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australia's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  governor \f28 \uc2 \f2 Glenn Stevens\f28 \uc2 \f2  is flying in a holding pattern, waiting for signs the economy is recovering after a slowdown in the once-booming resources sector.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  slashed interest rates to a record-low 2.5% last August as a long mining boom faded. The Reserve Bank has indicated rates will stay there for some time: many economists forecast the first rate hike won't come until 2015.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The country's jobless rate hit 6% in January, the highest level in more than a decade, as workers lost jobs in mining, oil and gas industries. Business conditions and consumer sentiment have also deteriorated recently.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The economy expanded by 2.8% in 2013, down from growth rates as high as 4% in early 2012.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Still, a string of more upbeat data, including rising house prices and a sharp pickup in building approvals, has buoyed hopes of a recovery in construction. Nationally, house prices rose almost 10% last year, the strongest gain since 2010.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said in March it was monitoring a surge in speculative lending for property, saying the sector by some measures is registering boom-like conditions. With roughly 40% of home loans now going to investors it is worried that unrealistic expectations of price gains are building. "We remind people that house prices can go down as well as up, " the bank said in March, in its twice-a-year review of financial stability.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  doesn't want to raise rates to dampen house prices, because that could undercut a revival of retail spending and construction.\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Brazil\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 By Paulo Trevisani\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Brazil\f28 \uc2 \f2  pressed on with rate hikes in the first quarter as inflation showed little sign of easing. The institution raised its benchmark Selic rate to 10.75%, with increases in January and February.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With inflation well above the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's 4.5% target, it is expected to raise the Selic again in early April. Many analysts expect the rate to reach at least 11% by yearend, though it may go higher. That could lower the outlook for economic growth, now a meager 1.69% this year.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  President Alexandre Tombini's team continued its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  through the use of derivatives contracts even though it says much of the impact on Brazil from the decision by the U.S. Federal Reserve to reduce its bond-buying program has been absorbed. As of late March, there were $87.2 billion of swap contracts outstanding, up from $75.1 billion at the end of 2013.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Brazilian real rallied in March, ending the month at around BRL2.27 per dollar from above BRL2.40 in January. Such strength helps the inflation battle but may hurt exports, further dampening growth, and some believe the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  could look to curtail its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at some point.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bank of Canada\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 By Nirmala Menon\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Canada\f28 \uc2 \f2  has kept interest-rate cuts on the table amid persistent low inflation. But if consumer prices pick up and a stronger U.S. economy lifts demand for Canadian exports, Governor Stephen Poloz might have to dial back the dovishness.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Mr. Poloz will have to be careful not to fire up the Canadian dollar in the process, jeopardizing a much-needed export revival that is expected to help drive Canadian growth this year. Mr. Poloz has described the weakening of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , which came after he abandoned the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's rate-hike bias last fall, as "icing" on the "cake" of improving U.S. demand.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 First-quarter inflation is already tracking higher than the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Canada\f28 \uc2 \f2  predicted in January, and may return to the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's 2% target as early as April, according to Jimmy Jean, economic strategist at Desjardins Capital Markets. Strengthening prices will force the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to put less emphasis on downside risks to inflation, or Mr. Poloz might be branded a "permanent dove," Mr. Jean said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Canada\f28 \uc2 \f2  may raise concerns if signs re-emerge about a dangerous household debt buildup, but it is likely to leave it to the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Canadian government\f28 \uc2 \f2  to handle potential risks on that front. Ottawa tightened mortgage insurance rules four times between 2008 and 2012 to cool the housing market and rein in household debt.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Mr. Poloz is expected to pull the rate-hike trigger next year at about the same time as the U.S. Federal Reserve if domestic consumer prices and exports keep strengthening. But he may wait until 2016 if the expected shift to an exports-driven economic expansion remains elusive.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Czech National Bank\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 By Leos Rousek\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Czech \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's regime of super low interest rates and a weak koruna are likely to stay in place until early 2015 and possibly beyond as slack consumer demand keeps the country's annual inflation rate well below the 2% target.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Speaking after the late March meeting of the bank's monetary policy board, Governor Miroslav Singer said the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is ready to extend its weak-koruna policy for even a longer period if the economic recovery remains fragile and more easing is needed.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Even if the bank starts winding down its weak-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  regime next year, it is unlikely to raise its benchmark interest rate from a record low of 0.05% at the same time, some analysts expect.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The bank is likely to deliver its first rate hike in the first quarter of 2016, or more than three years after cutting the headline interest rate to almost zero, said Jiri Skop, an analyst at the Czech unit of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Societe Generale\f28 \uc2 \f2  bank.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Seeking to counter the threat of deflation--or a period of falling prices--the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Czech \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has pledged to keep the koruna at around 27.00 to the euro, or about 6% weaker than before launching its EUR7.4 billion ($10.2 billion) \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in November.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Danish National Bank\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 By Charles Duxbury\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  officials in Copenhagen will be keeping a close eye on Frankfurt over the next few months.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Denmark's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has been watching the krone's value closely to meet a target of exchange rate stability against the euro.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A recent weakening of the Danish \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  got analysts wondering whether the bank might \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market in the short term and even raise rates in the medium term, but such action hasn't been taken.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 For now the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's ultra-low policy rates--which include a negative rate on bank deposits--are likely to remain stable. But if the euro shows signs of moving higher or lower over the coming months the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Danish \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  can be expected to react quickly.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In that sense, Denmark is heavily dependent on what the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  does, even though Denmark isn't a member of the euro. If the ECB installs a negative rate on bank deposits, weakening the euro, it could force Denmark to respond with steps to cheap its own \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , including moving its deposit rate--currently 0.1%--further into negative territory.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 After all, it was a rise in the value of the krone against the euro, as investors sought a safe have in Denmark during the worst of the euro zone crisis, that triggered the cut to a negative deposit rate in the first place back in July 2012.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1 Apr 2014 16:18 ET \par Global Central Banking in 2014, A First Quarter -3-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We will do whatever it takes to protect the peg," Governor Lars Rohde said in an interview last year. "The one and only role for Danish monetary policy today is to secure the peg."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Hungarian National Bank\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 By Margit Feher\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Hungary's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is likely to reduce its benchmark interest rate from a record low of 2.6% early in the second quarter, extending an period of rate cuts that has already run over 20 months.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Following their most recent decision to cut interest rates in March, policy makers said that only a marked deterioration in sentiment in international markets would stand in the way of further easing.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Analysts warn that while further cuts will have little impact on growth, they could threaten financial stability, raise the country's financing costs, and weaken the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But with an election approaching, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is committed to doing what it can to support feeble but accelerating growth.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A weaker forint would raise the debt payment obligations of the about half a million Hungarian households holding mortgages tied mostly to the Swiss franc, and also that of the government, 40% of whose debt is denominated in a foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has become increasingly tolerant of a weaker forint and some analysts reckon that may foreshadow a government effort--if the current governing Fidesz party wins a second consecutive term in power in line with the latest opinion polls--to try to boost growth later this year with an even softer \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of India\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 By Raymond Zhong\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of India Gov. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Raghuram Rajan\f28 \uc2 \f2  started the year with a jolt, raising interest rates in January to 8% from 7.75% in a surprise move that briefly sent stocks tumbling. For now, Mr. Rajan's shock tactics seem to be paying off.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Inflation in Asia's third-largest economy eased in January and February, after both consumer- and producer-price growth accelerated in the second half of last year despite slower economic growth. The rupee, after an embattled 2013, has stabilized against the U.S. dollar. Imports have fallen and the trade gap has narrowed.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 All of this has helped repair investor confidence in India, which still depends on inflows of foreign capital to fund trade and government-budget deficits. At the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's April meeting, its second of the year, it left interest rates unchanged, citing a "sizable fall" in headline inflation.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But Mr. Rajan's hawkish leanings aren't universally beloved.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  also said at its April meeting that it will officially use consumer-price inflation as a guide for monetary policy. For now, the objectives are 8% inflation by January 2015 and 6% by January 2016--a so-called "glide path" for inflation rather than a long-term target. But that still marks a shift away from the RBI's previous approach of looking at more than one indicator--inflation, growth, exchange rates and others--when it sets its policy rate. The move suggests the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will look to keep monetary policy tight for the next few months until inflation is brought down.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Critics of the inflation targets argue that a developing country like India can't afford to fetishize price stability at the expense of economic growth.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \uc2 \f2 International Monetary Fund\f28 \uc2 \f2  expects India's economy to expand by 5.4% in 2014. That's up from 4.4% last year but still far off the 7.7% average yearly growth of the decade before. Mr. Rajan has said repeatedly that he doesn't believe there's a tradeoff between inflation and growth.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bank Indonesia\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 By I Made Sentana\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Indonesia's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has kept policy on hold since December, but officials warned they would raise interest rates if capital outflows resume or domestic inflation remains above the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's year-end target of 5.5%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We don't want to be complacent," Senior Deputy Gov. Mirza Adityaswara said March 24.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Indonesia has attracted some $5.5 billion of foreign funds into its capital markets so far this year, pushing up the rupiah more than 7% and making it the best performer among emerging-market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bank Indonesia Gov. Agus Martowardojo warned that Indonesia's current-account deficit, the broadest measure of trade, could widen again in the second quarter due to offshore debt repayment and profit repratriation by multinational companies operating in Indonesia. If that happens, he said, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank Indonesia\f28 \uc2 \f2  would again prioritize "stability over growth."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Mr. Adityaswara said inflation, which had declined since last summer to 7.75% in February, could pick up again later this year if drier weather expected to affect Southeast Asia drives up food prices. Higher electricity prices also could stoke inflation.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bank of Israel\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 By Josh Mitnick\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Israeli \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  governor Karnit Flug didn't wait long to surprise observers on interest rate policy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Despite expectations that she would keep monetary policy unchanged for the first half of 2014, she cut the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's lending rate by 0.25 percentage point in February to boost economic growth.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 For the remainder of 2014, the outlook for stable rates has not changed, say economists. Cutting rates further may spur an already frothy real-estate sector. But raising them may boost Israel's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the shekel, and weaken the country's dominant export sector.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It's very boring, but I think that's the most reasonable forecast at the moment," said Jonathan Katz, economist at Leider Holdings and Investments.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Economic activity has started to pick up, making additional rate cuts less urgent. Ms. Flug may also be cautious due to uncertainty over a new plan by Finance Minister Yair Lapid to give tax relief to would-be home buyers struggling amid the country's surging real estate market. The concern about the yet-to-be approved policy is that the tax break will kick up more demand amid a housing shortage, boosting prices even more.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 There is an outside chance for a rate cut if the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  chief is concerned that Israel's export sector is being hurt by the strong shekel, said Vered Dar, chief of the investment committee at Excellence Nessuah Investment House.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The shekel is way too strong, and currently that's what \f28 \uc2 \f2 the Bank of Israel\f28 \uc2 \f2  is most concerned about," she said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of New Zealand\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 By Rebecca Howard\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 New Zealand's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in March raised interest rates for the first time in almost four years, breaking from other developed nations that are still emerging from the global financial crisis.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The reasoning was straightforward. After three years at a record-low 2.5%, rates were simply too low for an economy steaming ahead, stoked by growing Chinese demand for dairy products and a construction boom as rebuilding intensifies after a 2011 earthquake. Consumer sentiment and retail sales are robust and employment is improving.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It is necessary to raise interest rates towards a level at which they are no longer adding to demand," Reserve Bank Gov. Graeme Wheeler said March 13. "New Zealand's economic expansion has considerable momentum, and growth is becoming more broad-based."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The quarter percentage-point hike, to 2.75%, is expected to be the first of many: The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of New Zealand\f28 \uc2 \f2  is unusually open about its plans. The 90-day bank bill track, widely considered a proxy for interest rates, shows it plans to raise interest rates by around 2 percentage points over the next two years.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The rate increase, while expected, sent the nation's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to its highest level against a basket of major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  since it was floated in 1985--underscoring the risks to the export-dependent economy of acting ahead of the pack.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Norges Bank\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 By Charles Duxbury\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Norway is an anomaly among developed economies in Europe, the U.S. and Asia. Inflation is near its target, and while other central banks protect fragile recoveries and guard against too-low inflation, Norway expects a soft landing.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As a result, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Norges Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  has kept a low profile over recent months sticking to a policy mix of unchanged rates and regular postponements of rate rises which it signals explicitly with its forward guidance. The benchmark interest rate has been at 1.5% for two years now.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The bank stuck largely to its tried and tested script in a statement March 27 but reiterated its plan to raise rates next summer rather than move higher borrowing costs further into the future.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Given our rate path, we'll maybe be a little bit earlier than some other central banks in hiking rates," the bank's governor Oeystein Olsen told The Wall Street Journal.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The market found that it a bit hawkish and the krone rose a little against the euro. Still the Norwegian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  remains well below the highs it hit when investors were seeking safe places to invest during the worst of Europe's recent economic crisis.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 There is little reason to think that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will change policy over the months ahead as its forecasts for slow but steady growth and inflation close to its 2.5% target are proving close to the mark.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A rapid appreciation of the krone in the wake of further ECB easing could change the balance, but for now \f28 \uc2 \f2 Norges Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  is on autopilot.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 National Bank of Poland\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 By Patryk Wasilewski\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Poland's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is expected to keep borrowing costs at an all-time-low longer than believed likely only a few months ago amid uncertainty about economic impact of a territorial dispute between the country's Eastern neighbors Russia and Ukraine.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A dovish shift in the bank's 10-strong rate setting panel was clearly visible in recent weeks with most members arguing there is no need to change rates this year as the conflict over Crimea could potentially dent Polish exports to its eastern neighbors and hit growth prospects in the second half of the year.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Monetary Policy Committee member Andrzej Bratkowski is the exception, arguing that a rate increase is still possible in the fourth quarter of this year if the economy shows signs of potential inflation pressures.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1 Apr 2014 16:18 ET \par Global Central Banking in 2014, A First Quarter -4-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In March the panel surprised the market with a promise to keep the benchmark interest rate unchanged at current 2.5% level at least until September after its inflation and growth forecasts showed the economy doesn't need any additional monetary stimulus.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 According to the updated forecasts, the annual rate of inflation will reach the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's 2.5% target sometime in 2016 while economic growth is seen accelerating to 3.6% in 2014 and 3.7% in 2015 from 1.6% in 2013 and remaining only slightly below what the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sees as the country's inflation-neutral growth rate.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bank of Russia\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 By Andrey Ostroukh\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Ukrainian crisis became a major game-changer for the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Russia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , forcing it to step up \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market and tighten monetary policy despite flagging economic growth.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  had been widely expected to gradually ease monetary policy this year and grant the ruble more freedom before fully switching to inflation targeting policy in early 2015.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But then the crisis in Ukraine escalated. On March 3, when the ruble dropped to all-time lows, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  unexpectedly raised interest rates by 150 basis points and changed the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  mechanism, selling more than $11 billion in just one day to limit the fall.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is expected to change course and cut rates while trimming the amount of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  once the Ukraine-related market turmoil subsides. Given the fluid nature of the crisis, changes to the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy are possible at any time, including April 25 when the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has long been planning to hold a rate-setting meeting.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 According to the economy ministry, Russia's economy may grow just 0.6% this year. It could shrink 1.8% if tensions escalate and sanctions bite enough to push net capital outflow to $150 billion, more than double last year's pace.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Rate cuts may help, but the case isn't clear cut. Global investors have pulled capital from Russia and lower interest rates could make matters worse.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bank of Russia officials say they will still stick to their plan to stop targeting the ruble exchange rate and begin targeting inflation in 2015. But the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves a right to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in order to support financial stability if the exchange rate drops dramatically.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South African Reserve Bank\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 By Patrick McGroarty\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The first quarter was a turbulent one for \f28 \uc2 \f2 South Africa's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . It was at the center of the financial turmoil that gripped many emerging markets in January and forced it to hike a key lending rate from a four-decade low of 5%, to 5.5%, to keep capital from fleeing the country.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A pause at the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's March rate-setting meeting, amid recent calm in emerging markets, left some economists worried that additional rate increases might not come fast enough to draw investors back to a beleaguered \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank Governor Gill Marcus said on March 27 that the bank was leaving rates on hold because economic growth keeps falling short of the bank's forecasts--she cut her growth outlook for this year to 2.6%, from 2.8% previously.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But she said persistent weakness in South Africa's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the rand, and forecasts for the inflation rate to push above the bank's 6% target ceiling in the second quarter meant the bank would have to push rates up sometime soon.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We are indicating that interest rates are likely to rise in the future, " she said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Many economists expect another two or more rate increases to at least 6.5% sometime next year.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A lot depends on the U.S. If the Federal Reserve raises interest rates sooner than expected, South Africa might be forced to make robust rate hikes to help the rand bounce back from a 20% tumble against the dollar in the last year.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "South Africa's ability to sustain an ultra-accommodative monetary policy, in order to help the economic recovery along, will be limited," said Razia Khan, head of Africa research at Standard Chartered.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bank of Korea\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 By Kwanwoo Jun\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The big question in South Korea is whether the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will raise rates this year after keeping its policy rate steady at 2.5% for 10 months through March.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of Korea\f28 \uc2 \f2 's new governor, Lee Ju-yeol, 61, a career central banker, takes the helm in April.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Some observers say a rate increase is now possible in coming months.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 An improving economic picture in the U.S. has raised expectations of higher U.S. rates in the future and a strengthening dollar against Korea's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the won.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 That could push the BOK to raise rates to stem capital outflows and to ensure inflation doesn't pick up via higher prices of imports.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Korea's export-led economy also is set to expand at a faster rate as demand in industrialized nations recovers. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  expects growth of 3.8% in 2014, up from 3.0% growth last year, and inflation to accelerate to 2.3% from 1.3% in 2013.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Nomura economist Kwon Young-sun says a weaker won could boost exports and growth, pushing the BOK to raise its base rate in September.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But \f28 \uc2 \f2 J.P. Morgan\f28 \uc2 \f2  economists don't expect a rate hike in 2014. They say large capital outflows are unlikely given Korea's stable balance of payments, even if U.S. yields rise.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The bank also points out that any depreciation in the won will be matched by declines in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Korea's main trading partners in Asia. On a trade-weighted basis, the won is unlikely to fall much, the bank surmises.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Sweden's Riksbank\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 By Charles Duxbury\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Sweden's Riksbank has started 2014 where it ended 2013 - in a policy dilemma.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Inflation is low and household debt levels are high and rising. This is pulling the six-member \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy board in different directions on what to do with borrowing costs.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Riksbank's forecasts from February signal unchanged rates until early next year and analysts still see this to be the most likely scenario.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Inflation in January and February stayed low. But the inflation outlook is probably not bleak enough to trigger a rate cut at the next policy meeting in April, analysts say.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Still the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may choose to signal that rate hikes will be longer in coming than previously expected as a way to stimulate the economy and push up inflationary pressures.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Another development to watch: a split on the six-member Riksbank board. Some, including Governor Stefan Ingves, are reluctant to cut the benchmark rate below its current 0.75%. They think such a move would send a signal to already heavily indebted households that they can borrow even more and that this could increase risks in the financial sector.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A dovish camp, led by Karolina Ekholm, counter that the Riksbank must pay more intention to the fact that consumer price inflation is near zero and the bank's inflation target set by lawmakers is 2%. She recently said she wouldn't rule out calling for further rate cuts if inflation doesn't pick up.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A four-two division on the issue was entrenched for many months but has recently shown signs of breaking down.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Specifically, one of the more hawkish four, Per Jansson, has since December expressed much more concern about low inflation and said this is now his priority.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Swiss National Bank\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 By Neil MacLucas\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Swiss National Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  has kept monetary policy--including interest rates near zero and a ceiling for the franc-euro exchange rate--on hold for more than two and a half years, and that won't change anytime soon. But this doesn't mean the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will be on autopilot.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 If the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  takes dramatic steps that weaken the euro--such as negative interest rates or large-scale asset purchases--the Swiss ceiling of CHF1.20 per euro may be further tested. That may force the SNB to again purchase foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to keep the franc from rising too much, something it hasn't had to do since September 2012.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \uc2 \f2 International Monetary Fund\f28 \uc2 \f2  said recently the SNB may need to consider setting a negative rate on bank deposits. SNB Chairman Thomas Jordan said that was a possibility, in an interview with The Wall Street Journal.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  imposed its franc-euro ceiling in September 2011 to head off the threat of deflation and relieve exporters under pressure from the rise of the franc to near parity with the euro, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of the biggest Swiss export market. The franc was about CHF1.22 per euro at the end of March, buoyed as the crisis in the Ukraine and Crimea triggered safe haven demand for the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . The relatively high value of the franc means cheaper imported goods, which has resulted in mild deflation. Switzerland's overall price level dropped by 0.1% in February, while the SNB's definition of price stability is a rate of less than 2%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Still, economists don't expect any change to SNB policy until late 2015\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of The Republic of Turkey\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 By Emre Peker\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Turkey's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  entered 2014 battling international and domestic pressures that pushed it in opposite directions.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Its government wanted easier monetary policies to boost growth ahead of elections. International investors wanted higher rates to stabilize the lira and keep capital from fleeing the country.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors won that round after Turkey emerged as the epicenter of a rout in emerging markets at the start of the year.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Policymakers in Ankara more than doubled a key interest rate to 10% at an extraordinary gathering just a week after their January meeting, firmly establishing inflation as their main concern amid the lira's 30% plunge against the dollar that started fueling price increases across the board.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But the move was also criticized as too late, with Turkey's inflation hitting 7.89% in February, rising for a fourth consecutive month and moving further away from the 5% official target.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Central bankers have pledged to keep money tight until they see a "significant improvement in the inflation outlook."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1 Apr 2014 16:18 ET \par Global Central Banking in 2014, A First Quarter -5-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 That could change later this year, with looser policies to boost economic growth forecasts, which were halved to about 2% after the emergency rate hike, analysts said. The policies probably won't involve the key interest rates amid uncertainty over U.S. Federal Reserve policies, which play a key role in Turkey's financial markets and economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Rather, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  might start paying banks interest on deposits used to meet reserve requirements. Lenders are also pressing the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to cut down reserve requirements. Another option is reducing the amount of money banks need to set aside for loans.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 These backdoor measures would raise liquidity, ease the burden on Turkey's banks and boost the economy by supporting loans and credit card spending, economists said. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  More at The Wall Street Journal's Real Time Economics blog, \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://blogs.wsj.com/economics/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://blogs.wsj.com/economics/}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 April 01, 2014 16:18 ET (20:18 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140401ea41003ac\par }\par \b \uc2 Zusammenfassung der Suche\b0\par \par  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9072\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \uc2 Text\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \li50\uc2 (foreign exchange or fx or forex or currenc*) and (interven* or operation*) and (Russia near10 interven*) and (rst=trtw or rst=tprw or rst=tdjw) and (central bank or ministry of finance or treasury ministry or monetary authority)\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adju